! ============================================================================
!  MINGUS II: EVERYTHING ROLLS DOWNHILL  --  Frotzsmith sample (Inform 6 Std)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  The third longer sample, and the sequel to Mingus I. The Architect's
!  Legacy showed how a big game is ORGANISED; Mingus I showed how to make a
!  timed, deadly rule FAIR. This one keeps that layout and adds two lessons:
!
!    1. HOW TO CODE AN NPC. Lucan Fairweather, the Thursday tenor, follows
!       you, leads you, talks, reacts to what you give and show him, runs on
!       a small state machine, and dies in a scripted event on a timer. His
!       code is section 11, and every routine there opens with the LESSON it
!       teaches. Two more NPCs show two more patterns: Sandwich, a trickster
!       on a fixed schedule, and Mrs. Thrumble, who answers a bell.
!    2. HOW TO MAKE A VEHICLE WITHOUT MOVING ROOMS. The funeral ship is four
!       fixed rooms. Where the ship IS lives in one variable, ship_at, which
!       decides what you see over the rail, where OUT leads, and what the
!       current does.
!
!  THE STORY
!    Mingus I ended at the foot of a funeral ship's gangplank, with thousands
!    of verdlings bowed to the pike in your hand. Mingus II starts there, the
!    next morning. Nobody who has floated down the Verdance has ever come
!    back. On the way down you find five names -- evidence of how far five of
!    the lost got -- and stow them in the ship's custody chest. The river
!    decides how far YOU get.
!
!  HOW THIS FILE IS ORGANISED  (search for the banner text to jump)
!     1. SETUP ............ story constants, the score table, ALL game state
!     2. LIBRARY .......... Parser + VerbLib includes
!     3. CLASSES .......... Room, ShipRoom, Feature, Person, LostName
!     4. HELPERS .......... the ship's position, casting off, the river rule
!     5. THE SHIP ......... Main Deck, Prow, Stern, Hold  (rooms 1-4)
!     6. PART ONE ......... The Sluice          (the Mingus bank, rooms 5-7)
!     7. PART TWO ......... The Sandbar         (mid-river, rooms 8-9)
!     8. PART THREE ....... The old-side bank   (rooms 10-14)
!     9. PART FOUR ........ The Gilded Span     (mid-river, rooms 15-16)
!    10. PART FIVE ........ The Intake          (the Mingus bank, rooms 17-19)
!    11. NPCS ............. Lucan (the showcase), Sandwich, Mrs. Thrumble
!    12. THE DISPATCHER ... the one end-of-turn daemon: river, Lucan, Sandwich
!    13. FINALE ........... The Old-Side Shore  (room 20)
!    14. ENTRY POINTS ..... Initialise, DeathMessage, PrintRank, PrintTaskName
!    15. GRAMMAR .......... Grammar include + the game's own verbs
!
!  THE MAP  (20 rooms; the river runs SOUTH, the Mingus bank is west, the
!            old-side bank east)
!
!    PART ONE  Inside the Sluice -w/e- Sluice Mouth -w/e- Foot of the Gangplank
!                                                            | up/in/board
!    THE SHIP (four fixed rooms)                         Main Deck --n/s-- Prow
!                                                            |  \--s/n-- Stern
!                                                            | down/up
!                                                           Hold [custody chest]
!
!    OUT from the Main Deck (and IN back) depends on ship_at:
!      SANDBAR  The Sandbar -e/w- Expedition Wreck
!      PIER     Stone Pier -n/s- Mine Heads -n/s- Hollow Green -n/s- Old Fence
!                                    | e/w
!                              Outside the Half-Buried Hall
!      SPAN     (Prow) -up/climb chain/down- Broken End -w/e- Span Deck
!      INTAKE   Intake Screen -w/e- Divers' Shed ; Intake Screen -n/s- Drowned
!                                                                      Road
!    FINALE    (the Race) --wreck--> The Old-Side Shore
!
!    The voyage: Sluice -> passage 1 -> Sandbar -> passage 2 -> Stone Pier ->
!    passage 3 -> Gilded Span -> passage 4 -> Intake -> passage 5, the Race
!    -> wrecked on the old-side bank.
!
!  THE PUZZLES  (10 puzzles x 8 points + 5 names x 4 points = 100)
!    P1  The launch       bow first, then the lever  -> BOW, PULL LEVER
!    P2  The feint        reach for the gilt         -> TAKE STRONGBOX
!    P3  Ship the sweep   a steering oar             -> PUT SWEEP IN SOCKET
!    P4  Kedge off        grapnel astern, haul       -> THROW GRAPNEL, PULL LINE
!    P5  The crossing     the one-turn window        -> STEER EAST
!    P6  The lifebelt     where Lucan leads you      -> TAKE LIFEBELT
!    P7  The chain        the one-turn window        -> THROW GRAPNEL AT CHAIN
!    P8  The civic key    a long reach               -> PULL BOX WITH PIKE
!    P9  Stop the pump    Vaunt's key, Vaunt's pump  -> PUT KEY IN SWITCH
!    P10 The Race         wear what floats           -> WEAR LIFEBELT
!    N1-N5 The five names, each PUT IN CHEST: the launch register, the
!    Expedition's nameboard, Lucan's songbook, the diver's tag, and
!    Councillor Sutt's plaque.
!
!  TECHNIQUES WORTH STEALING
!    - ONE end-of-turn dispatcher (world_clock, section 12) that runs the
!      river, then Lucan, then Sandwich, in a fixed order. No other daemon,
!      timer or each_turn prints anything, so every turn's text always comes
!      out in the same order.
!    - A vehicle as state: four ship rooms that never move, plus ship_at,
!      passage and drift for "where is the ship?" (section 4).
!    - A countdown with a window: the current. A landing comes alongside
!      and you get exactly one turn to act. A miss loops you round (the
!      eddy) or kills you (the broken end).
!    - NPC: a state machine (HIDDEN -> FOLLOWING -> GUIDING -> ARRIVED ->
!      DEAD), and each state's behaviour in one place (LucanTick).
!    - NPC: following (he walks after you) and leading (he goes first, waits
!      a turn for you, and catches up if you get ahead).
!    - NPC: conversation: one topic table shared by ASK and TELL, a fallback
!      line, and a greeting (HELLO, SAY HELLO, GREET LUCAN).
!    - NPC: reacting to GIVE and SHOW, item by item.
!    - NPC: a scripted, timed event (his death, two turns after arrival,
!      whatever you do), and its consequences: his effects stay where he
!      fell, another NPC mentions him, and his book reaches you through her.
!    - A scheduled antagonist whose interference is deterministic and
!      learnable (Sandwich's front-run rule).
!    - Gates that explain themselves: you can't leave a landing without the
!      thing you'll need next, and the refusal says why.
!    - A container that only takes some things and never gives back (the
!      custody chest, which works like a trophy case).
!    - A warn-once global rule (the river).
!    - A win and a death that end with different words (deadflag 3 vs 1),
!      and a custom rank (PrintRank) that carries the closing teaser.
!
!  Compiles cleanly with the bundled Standard Library 6.12; targets z5.
! ============================================================================


! ============================================================================
!  1. SETUP  --  story constants, the score table, and all game state
! ============================================================================

Constant Story      "Mingus II: Everything Rolls Downhill";
Constant Headline   "^A Frotzsmith sample: a river voyage in five landings.^";

! --- Scoring ----------------------------------------------------------------
! The library's task system: task_scores holds what each task is worth, and
! Achieved(task) awards it exactly once -- so it is always safe to call it
! again. FULL SCORE names completed tasks via PrintTaskName (section 14).

Constant TASKS_PROVIDED;
Constant NUMBER_TASKS  15;
Constant MAX_SCORE     100;

Constant TASK_LAUNCH     0;   ! P1  the launch: bow, then the lever
Constant TASK_FEINT      1;   ! P2  Sandwich front-runs the strongbox
Constant TASK_SWEEP      2;   ! P3  the sweep shipped in its socket
Constant TASK_KEDGE      3;   ! P4  kedged off the Sandbar
Constant TASK_CROSSING   4;   ! P5  steered across to the old side
Constant TASK_LIFEBELT   5;   ! P6  the lifebelt, where Lucan led you
Constant TASK_CHAIN      6;   ! P7  the grapnel on the chain
Constant TASK_KEY        7;   ! P8  the civic key, fished off the girder
Constant TASK_PUMP       8;   ! P9  the pump stopped
Constant TASK_RACE       9;   ! P10 the Race survived
Constant TASK_REGISTER  10;   ! N1  the launch register, in the chest
Constant TASK_NAMEBOARD 11;   ! N2  the Expedition's nameboard
Constant TASK_SONGBOOK  12;   ! N3  Lucan's songbook
Constant TASK_TAG       13;   ! N4  the diver's tag
Constant TASK_PLAQUE    14;   ! N5  Councillor Sutt's plaque

!                   P1 P2 P3 P4 P5 P6 P7 P8 P9 P10 | N1 N2 N3 N4 N5
Array task_scores -> 8  8  8  8  8  8  8  8  8  8     4  4  4  4  4;

! --- Where the ship is -------------------------------------------------------
! ship_at is one of these. The landings are numbered from 1 so that 0 can
! mean "this room is not part of any landing" (a Room's `landing`, below).
Constant SLUICE   1;          ! moored at the Sluice      (Part One)
Constant SANDBAR  2;          ! aground on the Sandbar    (Part Two)
Constant PIER     3;          ! made fast at the Stone Pier (Part Three)
Constant SPAN     4;          ! hanging from the Span's chain (Part Four)
Constant INTAKE   5;          ! pinned to the intake screen (Part Five)
Constant ADRIFT   6;          ! on the current, between landings

! --- Lucan's states (section 11 explains each one) ---------------------------
Constant HIDDEN    0;         ! stowed away; not in play yet
Constant FOLLOWING 1;         ! walks after you
Constant GUIDING   2;         ! goes ahead of you on the old-side bank
Constant ARRIVED   3;         ! at the Hollow Green; the timer is running
Constant DEAD      4;         ! gone; only his effects remain

! --- Sandwich's schedule (section 11) ----------------------------------------
Constant SW_UPSTREAM 0;       ! offshore at the Foot of the Gangplank
Constant SW_AHEAD    1;       ! shot past you; waiting at the Wreck
Constant SW_WAITING  2;       ! has told you the front-run rule
Constant SW_HOLDING  3;       ! holds a decoy: "Yours for the coat."
Constant SW_INTAKE   4;       ! pinned at the intake, holding the plaque
Constant SW_RACING   5;       ! rowed off downstream ahead of you
Constant SW_GONE     6;       ! broke up in the white water

! --- Game state -------------------------------------------------------------
! Everything the game remembers lives here. Ask "what has the player done?"
! and the answer is on this screen.

Global ship_at        = SLUICE;   ! where the ship is (see the constants)
Global passage        = 0;        ! which passage, 1-5, while ADRIFT
Global drift          = 0;        ! turns into this passage (0-3)
Global lucan_state    = HIDDEN;   ! Lucan's state machine
Global lucan_timer    = 0;        ! turns since he ARRIVED at the Hollow Green
Global last_location  = 0;        ! where you were when the last turn ended
Global sandwich_state = SW_UPSTREAM;
Global river_warned   = false;    ! the Verdance has used up its one warning
Global pump_stopped   = false;    ! P9 done: the intake has let go
Global lines_let_go   = false;    ! P1: the verdlings have let go the lines
Global sweep_shipped  = false;    ! P3: the sweep is in the stern socket
Global kedge_set      = false;    ! P4: the grapnel is biting astern
Global grapnel_hooked = false;    ! P7: the grapnel holds the chain
Global songbook_given = false;    ! Mrs. Thrumble has handed over the songbook


! ============================================================================
!  2. LIBRARY
! ============================================================================

Include "Parser";
Include "VerbLib";


! ============================================================================
!  3. CLASSES  --  write shared behaviour once
! ============================================================================

! Room: every location. All are lit (daylight, even in the Hold, through the
! deck seams), so darkness never gets in the way.
!   landing    -- which landing this room belongs to (SLUICE ... INTAKE), or
!                 0. Lucan's following rule and the cast-off checks use it.
!   riverside  -- true where the Verdance is right there: SWIM, DIVE and
!                 JUMP here are RIVER ATTEMPTS (section 4).
!   river_line -- the room's own line for the first river attempt.
! `before` is an ADDITIVE property: a room's own `before` runs first, and if
! it doesn't stop the action, this one runs too.
Class Room
  with landing 0,
       riverside false,
       river_line "You stand at the edge, and the Verdance goes by, quick and
           quiet, close enough to touch. You step back.",
       before [;
         Swim, Jump:
           if (self.riverside) return RiverAttempt(self.river_line);
       ],
  has  light;

! ShipRoom: the four rooms of the funeral ship. They never move; ship_at says
! where they are. OUT is the same routine in all four (ShipOut, section 4),
! and the river is always over the rail.
Class ShipRoom
  class Room
  with riverside true,
       river_line "You put a leg over the rail, and the current pulls at your
           boot like a hand. You climb back.",
       out_to [; return ShipOut(); ];

! Feature: things a description mentions that you can examine but that the
! library should neither list nor let you carry off.
Class Feature
  with before [;
         Take, Pull, Push, Turn:
           print_ret (The) self, " ", (IsOrAre) self, " not coming with
               you.";
       ],
  has  scenery;

! Person: the characters. `life` is ADDITIVE too: each character's own life
! routine handles what it cares about and returns false for anything else,
! which falls through to these shared fallbacks.
!   greeting -- what HELLO, SAY HELLO and GREET get from this person
!               (HelloSub, section 15, finds the person present).
Class Person
  with greeting "There is no answer.",
       no_answer "There is no answer.",
       life [;
         Ask, Tell, Answer:
           PrintOrRun(self, no_answer);
           rtrue;
         Kiss:
           "This is not that kind of river.";
       ],
  has  animate;

! LostName: the five names you are collecting. Each knows the task it
! scores when it goes into the custody chest (section 5), and `reading` is
! the name Lucan reads aloud if you show it to him.
Class LostName
  with task 0,
       reading "a name";


! ============================================================================
!  4. HELPERS  --  the ship's position, casting off, the river rule
! ============================================================================

! --- The ship's position ------------------------------------------------------
! ShipTo(where): the one place ship_at changes. Objects with a `found_in`
! routine (the chain, the mooring rope, the verdlings on the bank...) decide
! where they are from ship_at, but the library only re-checks them when you
! move. MoveFloatingObjects() asks them again right now, so the room you are
! standing in is correct even though you didn't go anywhere.
[ ShipTo where;
    ship_at = where;
    MoveFloatingObjects();
];

! OutsideView: one sentence about the outside, chosen by ship_at. The Main
! Deck ends its description with it -- the whole "vehicle" is this switch.
[ OutsideView;
    switch (ship_at) {
      SLUICE:  print "Over the rail, the slipway runs down into the Verdance,
                   and the bowed verdlings line the Mingus bank, holding the
                   ship's lines.";
      SANDBAR: print "Over the rail, the ship sits aground on a long pale
                   sandbar in mid-river. You could go out onto it.";
      PIER:    print "Over the rail, the ship is made fast to an iron ring on
                   an old stone pier. That is the old side. You could go
                   out onto it.";
      SPAN:    print "Overhead hangs the broken end of the gilded span, and
                   the ship hangs from its chain by the grapnel line,
                   shuddering. The way up is the chain, from the prow.";
      INTAKE:  print "Over the rail, the gilded grating of the intake holds
                   the ship pinned against the Mingus bank. You could go out
                   onto its catwalk.";
      ADRIFT:  print "Over the rail, the Verdance goes by, quick and quiet.";
    }
];

! ShipOut: OUT from any ship room. It goes ashore only from the Main Deck,
! and only at a landing that has a shore. An exit routine returns a room to
! go there, or prints a refusal and returns true to stay put.
[ ShipOut;
    if (location ~= main_deck) "The way off the ship is from the main deck.";
    switch (ship_at) {
      SLUICE:  return gangplank_foot;
      SANDBAR: return the_sandbar;
      PIER:    return stone_pier;
      INTAKE:  return intake_screen;
      SPAN:    "The only way off is up the chain, from the prow.";
    }
    "The Verdance. You know the line about the Verdance.";
];

! --- Casting off ---------------------------------------------------------------
! CastOff(n): leave the landing and start passage n. Each landing's cast-off
! action (PULL LEVER, PULL LINE, UNTIE ROPE, UNHOOK GRAPNEL, and the intake
! letting go) prints its own text and then calls this. The current itself
! moves at the end of the turn (RiverTick, section 12), which is why the
! cast-off turn always ends at [CURRENT: 1/3].
!
! The five names are optional. Leaving a landing without its name only
! costs points, and the river says so once.
[ CastOff n  left;
    switch (ship_at) {
      SLUICE:  left = launch_register;
      SANDBAR: left = nameboard;
      PIER:    left = songbook;
      SPAN:    left = diver_tag;
      INTAKE:  left = sutt_plaque;
    }
    if (left && ~~Aboard(left)) print "^The river does not go back.^";
    passage = n;
    drift = 0;
    ShipTo(ADRIFT);
];

! Aboard(o): is o with you or on the ship? Climb the object tree from o to
! the room it is in; if the player was on the way, you carry it.
[ Aboard o;
    while (parent(o) ~= 0) {
        if (o == player) rtrue;
        o = parent(o);
    }
    return (o ofclass ShipRoom);
];

! PrintCurrent: the counter, in plain ASCII, on a line of its own.
[ PrintCurrent;
    print "[CURRENT: ", drift, "/3]^";
];

! --- The river rule (warn once, globally) ---------------------------------------
! A RIVER ATTEMPT is SWIM, DIVE or JUMP in a riverside room, ENTER RIVER, and
! a few places' own ways in (the girder at the Broken End, the road at the
! Drowned Road). The very first one in the whole game is refused, with the
! place's own line; every later one, anywhere, kills. One Global,
! river_warned, is the whole memory of the rule.
[ RiverAttempt line;
    if (~~river_warned) {
        river_warned = true;
        print (string) line;
        "^^[The Verdance warns you once. It has never warned anyone twice.]";
    }
    RiverDeath();
    rtrue;
];

! The death text, shared by the river rule and the Race without a lifebelt.
! deadflag 1 makes the library print "*** You have died ***".
[ RiverDeath;
    print "The Verdance takes you, quick and quiet, the way it has taken
        everyone. Nobody comes back.^";
    deadflag = 1;
];


! ============================================================================
!  5. THE SHIP  --  four fixed rooms (1-4)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  The ship never moves in the object tree. ship_at (section 4) says where it
!  is; OutsideView and ShipOut turn that into what you see and where OUT goes.
! ============================================================================

! --- The player's own belongings --------------------------------------------

! The pike: Mingus I's rules carry over. It can't be given away, put in
! anything or thrown, so it can never be lost for good; DROP leaves it where
! it falls. GIVE PIKE TO LUCAN is let through to Lucan, who refuses it in
! character (section 11): a `before` returning false lets the action go on.
Object pike "pike"
  with name 'pike' 'verdling^s' 'staff' 'spear' 'pole',
       description "A verdling's pike: a long pole of river wood, its point
           worn bright. Thousands of verdlings bowed to it. It is a very long
           reach.",
       before [;
         Give:
           if (second == lucan) rfalse;
           "You keep hold of the pike. Down here, it is the only thing anyone
           respects.";
         PutOn, Insert, ThrowAt:
           "You keep hold of the pike. Down here, it is the only thing anyone
           respects.";
       ];

! Lady Vaunt's coat, the real one. Nothing needs it; Sandwich will take it
! as a price (section 11), and Lucan knows it.
Object coat "Lady Vaunt's coat"
  with name 'coat' 'long' 'real' 'lady' 'vaunt^s' 'esme^s' 'shadow',
       description "Lady Vaunt's coat. The real one: it casts a shadow, which
           is more than most things from the Ascendancy do.",
  has  clothing proper;

! --- 1. Main Deck -------------------------------------------------------------

! The description is a routine because two things in it change: the
! receipt, and the last sentence, which is OutsideView (section 4).
ShipRoom main_deck "Main Deck"
  with description [;
           print "The main deck of a funeral ship: broad, gilded, and built
               for one passenger, who is meant to be lying down. An empty
               bier stands amidships under the mast, where a blank plaque is
               bolted ready for somebody's seed words. A receipt is pinned
               to the mast. Along the rail, the hooks where the hard wallets
               hung are all bare. A chock lever stands by the rail. The
               prow is north, the stern south, and a hatch leads down into
               the hold.^^";
           OutsideView();
           new_line;
       ],
       n_to prow,
       s_to stern,
       d_to hold;

Feature -> blank_plaque "blank plaque"
  with name 'blank' 'plaque' 'mast' 'seed' 'words',
       description "Blank. Somebody has polished it, ready.";

! Sandwich's first appearance is on paper: he got here before the dead did.
Object -> receipt "receipt"
  with name 'receipt' 'salvage' 'paper' 'note',
       description "SALVAGE RECEIPT. Item: hard wallets, all. Fee: everything.
           Thank you for choosing BOTTOM LINE SALVAGE. -- S.",
       before [;
         Take, Pull: "It's pinned to the mast with a gilt nail. Sandwich
             wanted it found.";
       ],
  has  static scenery;

Feature -> bier "empty bier"
  with name 'empty' 'bier' 'hooks' 'wallets' 'hard' 'rail',
       description "The bier is empty, and so are the hooks along the rail.
           The hard wallets are gone. Somebody got here first.";

Feature -> hatch "hatch"
  with name 'hatch' 'hatchway',
       description "A square hatch with a ladder, down into the hold.",
       before [;
         Enter: <<Go d_obj>>;
         Open, Close: "It stays open. The dead don't mind a draught.";
       ];

! P1, part two: the chock lever. PULL (or PUSH, TURN) does nothing until the
! verdlings have let go of the lines (BowSub, section 15). Then it launches
! the ship: the cast-off action for passage 1.
Object -> chock_lever "chock lever"
  with name 'chock' 'lever' 'handle',
       description [;
           if (ship_at == SLUICE) "A long iron lever. Below the rail it
               runs down to the chock that holds the ship on the slip.";
           "A long iron lever. Its chock is somewhere up the Verdance now.";
       ],
       before [;
         Pull, Push, Turn:
           if (ship_at ~= SLUICE) "The lever swings loose. Its chock is
               somewhere up the Verdance now, with everything else.";
           if (~~lines_let_go) "The ship strains against a thousand small
               hands on its lines, and does not move.";
           Achieved(TASK_LAUNCH);
           print "You pull the lever. Somewhere under the hull the chock
               knocks out, and the ship slides down the slip, gathering
               itself, and onto the Verdance. Behind you the verdlings
               stay bowed, and grow small.^";
           CastOff(1);
           rtrue;
       ],
  has  static scenery;

! --- 2. The Prow ----------------------------------------------------------------

! The chain (Part Four) is part of the view while it matters: it is named in
! the description during passage 3 and while the ship hangs from it. UP
! climbs it, but only when the ship is held at the Span.
ShipRoom prow "The Prow"
  with description [;
           print "The ship's high prow, where a gilded figurehead leans out
               over the water: Lord Vaunt, chin up, with laser eyes that
               stopped working some time ago. From here you see downstream:
               the Verdance, broad and green and going somewhere.";
           if (ship_at == SPAN) print " Overhead is the broken end of the
               span, and from it hangs the chain, with your grapnel bitten
               into it and the line bar-taut.";
           else if (ship_at == ADRIFT && passage == 3) print " Ahead, the
               gilded span reaches out from the Mingus bank and stops, and
               a chain hangs from its broken end toward the water.";
           "^^The main deck is back to the south.";
       ],
       s_to main_deck,
       u_to [;
           if (ship_at == SPAN) return broken_end;
           "Only the figurehead goes any higher, and it has never come
           down.";
       ];

Feature -> figurehead "figurehead"
  with name 'gilded' 'figurehead' 'vaunt' 'laser' 'eyes' 'head',
       description "Lord Vaunt in gilt, leaning into the wind. The laser eyes
           are dark. He is looking downstream, which is more than he ever
           did in life.";

! --- 3. The Stern ------------------------------------------------------------

! P3 lives here: the socket takes the sweep. STEER (section 15) works only
! here, and only with the sweep shipped. The kedge (P4) is thrown from here
! too, because the bar holds the bow and the deep water is astern.
ShipRoom stern "The Stern"
  with description [;
           print "The ship's stern, low and broad. In the deck is a socket,
               worn smooth, as if something used to sit in it, long ago,
               before anyone decided the dead didn't need to steer.";
           if (sweep_shipped) print " The sweep sits in it now, long and
               ready.";
           if (kedge_set) print " A line runs from the rail out into the
               water astern, where the grapnel is biting the riverbed.";
           "^^The main deck is north.";
       ],
       n_to main_deck;

! The socket takes one thing: the sweep. PUT SWEEP IN SOCKET sends the socket
! a ##Receive first, with `noun` = the thing being put in, so the socket
! decides -- before the library's own "that can't contain things".
Object -> socket "socket"
  with name 'socket' 'hole' 'worn',
       description [;
           if (sweep_shipped) "The sweep sits in it as if it had never
               left.";
           "A round socket in the stern deck, worn smooth. Something long
           used to sit in it.";
       ],
       before [;
         Receive:
           if (noun ~= sweep) "It was made for one thing, and that isn't
               it.";
           move sweep to stern;
           sweep_shipped = true;
           Achieved(TASK_SWEEP);
           "You set the sweep in the socket. It drops in as if it had been
           waiting.";
       ],
  has  static scenery;

! The kedge line exists only while the grapnel is biting astern. A
! `found_in` routine is asked "are you here?" whenever the player moves (or
! when ShipTo calls MoveFloatingObjects).
Object kedge_line "kedge line"
  with name 'line' 'kedge' 'rope',
       found_in [; return (kedge_set && location == stern); ],
       description "The grapnel's line, running from the rail into the deep
           water astern. It is taut.",
       before [;
         Pull, Take, Turn: return PullKedge();
       ],
  has  static scenery;

! --- 4. The Hold ------------------------------------------------------------

! The Hold: Lucan's hiding place, and the custody chest.
ShipRoom hold "The Hold"
  with description [;
           print "The hold, lit by daylight through the deck seams. Most of
               it is a heap of $ALT medallions, the ship's cargo of the
               dead's own money";
           if (lucan_state ~= HIDDEN) print ", with a hollow in it where
               Lucan slept, and a sandwich wrapper";
           ". Against the bulkhead stands the custody chest, stencilled
           MOST SECURE CUSTODY SOLUTION -- NOTHING HAS EVER BEEN WITHDRAWN.
           Its lid stands open. A ladder goes up to the main deck.";
       ],
       u_to main_deck;

! MEDALLION and MEDALLIONS: in a z5 story a dictionary word keeps only its
! first nine letters, so these two are the SAME word. Here that is fine --
! there is only one heap -- so neither ever asks "which do you mean?".
Object -> medallions "$ALT medallions"
  with name 'medallion' 'medallions' 'alt' 'heap' 'cargo' 'money' 'coins',
       description "Thousands of them, stamped with Vaunt's face and the
           word $ALT. They were the dead's own money. They are worth exactly
           what everything else from the Ascendancy is worth.",
       before [;
         Take, Pull, Push, Search: "They're $ALT. Leave them for the dead.";
       ],
  has  static scenery pluralname;

! The custody chest: the game's trophy case. It is an open container, but
! its `before` decides everything first:
!   ##Receive (PUT X IN CHEST, PUT X ON CHEST) -- only a LostName goes in,
!     and the first time it does, it scores its task.
!   ##LetGo (TAKE X, TAKE X FROM CHEST, for something inside) -- never.
! Because nothing ever comes out, a name can only go in once: no flag is
! needed to stop double scoring.
Object -> custody_chest "custody chest"
  with name 'custody' 'chest' 'lid' 'stencil' 'solution',
       description [;
           print "MOST SECURE CUSTODY SOLUTION -- NOTHING HAS EVER BEEN
               WITHDRAWN, says the stencil. The lid stands open.";
           if (child(self)) {
               print " Inside: ";
               WriteListFrom(child(self), ENGLISH_BIT);
               print ".";
           }
           new_line;
           rtrue;
       ],
       before [;
         Receive:
           if (~~(noun ofclass LostName)) "The chest is for names.";
           move noun to self;
           Achieved(noun.task);
           "The chest accepts it. NOTHING HAS EVER BEEN WITHDRAWN, says the
           lid.";
         LetGo:
           "NOTHING HAS EVER BEEN WITHDRAWN. The lid is very clear about
           this.";
         Close: "The lid doesn't close. It has nothing to hide, it says.";
       ],
  has  static container open scenery;


! Lucan's nest: only there once he has climbed out of it.
Object lucan_nest "Lucan's nest"
  with name 'nest' 'hollow' 'wrapper' 'sandwich',
       found_in [; return (lucan_state ~= HIDDEN && location == hold); ],
       description "A hollow in the medallions the shape of a large man, and a
           sandwich wrapper. Lucan travelled with what he could carry.",
       before [;
         Take, Search: "You leave it as it is.";
       ],
  has  static scenery;

! ============================================================================
!  6. PART ONE: THE SLUICE  --  the Mingus bank (rooms 5-7)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  The start. The launch drill (read at the Sluice Mouth) tells you the order
!  of P1: aboard, bow, lever. N1, the launch register, is inside the Sluice.
! ============================================================================

! --- 5. Foot of the Gangplank (start) -----------------------------------------

! `cant_go` replaces "You can't go that way" for every direction without an
! exit: the crowd is the wall here, as it was at the end of Mingus I.
Room gangplank_foot "Foot of the Gangplank"
  with landing SLUICE,
       riverside true,
       description "The foot of the gangplank, where Mingus I left you. The
           verdlings are still bowed, thousands of them, all the way back to
           the mountain, and the lane they made for you still runs to the
           water. At the end of it the funeral ship rocks at the mouth of
           the Sluice, its gangplank down, its blank plaque polished on the
           mast. The covered chute of the Sluice comes down out of the
           mountain to the west. Up the gangplank is the ship.",
       u_to main_deck,
       in_to main_deck,
       w_to sluice_mouth,
       cant_go "The crowd closes softly in front of you, and bows again.";

Feature -> lane "lane"
  with name 'lane' 'path' 'gap',
       description "A shoulder's width between bowed heads, running straight
           to the gangplank.";

! The ship seen from outside, at each landing it can be boarded from. ENTER
! SHIP and BOARD SHIP (section 15) walk aboard. UNTIE SHIP at the Pier means
! the mooring rope.
Object funeral_ship "funeral ship"
  with name 'ship' 'funeral' 'gangplank' 'hull' 'boat',
       found_in gangplank_foot the_sandbar stone_pier intake_screen,
       description "A funeral ship: a high gilded prow, a mast with a blank
           plaque, and a hold for the dead's money. It was built to carry
           one passenger, lying down.",
       before [;
         Enter, Climb: <<Go in_obj>>;
         Untie: if (location == stone_pier) <<Untie mooring_rope>>;
       ],
  has  scenery;

! The verdlings are on the Mingus bank only (they never cross the river and
! never go underground). They are in scope wherever you can see them,
! including from the Main Deck before the launch, so BOW TO VERDLINGS works
! there.
Object verdlings "verdlings"
  with name 'verdlings' 'verdling' 'crowd' 'heads' 'thousands' 'pikes',
       found_in [;
           if (location == gangplank_foot or sluice_mouth or intake_screen)
               rtrue;
           return (location == main_deck && ship_at == SLUICE);
       ],
       description [;
           if (location == intake_screen) "Far off along the Mingus bank, the
               verdlings stand in silent rows, watching. They keep well
               away from the intake. It is a hole.";
           if (location == sluice_mouth) "They keep well back from the
               Sluice. Nobody has told them why. Nobody had to.";
           "Thousands of small mud-coloured heads, bowed. Down by the water,
           a few hundred small hands hold the ship's lines.";
       ],
       life [;
         default: "Thousands of small heads stay bowed.";
       ],
  has  animate scenery pluralname;

! --- 6. The Sluice Mouth ------------------------------------------------------

Room sluice_mouth "The Sluice Mouth"
  with landing SLUICE,
       riverside true,
       description "The end of the covered chute, where the Sluice comes out
           of the mountain onto a stone slipway and the slipway runs down
           into the river. Funeral ships come down the chute on rollers and
           wait here on the chock for their last push. Painted on the arch
           above the chute is the launch drill. The verdlings keep well back.
           Inside the Sluice is west; the gangplank is east.",
       e_to gangplank_foot,
       w_to inside_sluice,
       in_to inside_sluice;

! The drill: READ is EXAMINE in the Standard Library, so a description is
! all a readable thing needs. It states P1's order outright.
Feature -> launch_drill "launch drill"
  with name 'launch' 'drill' 'arch' 'paint' 'order' 'painted',
       description "LAUNCH ORDER -- 1. THE PASSENGER ABOARD. 2. THE CHIEF
           BOWS; THE VERDLINGS LET GO. 3. THE CHOCK LEVER.";

Feature -> slipway "slipway"
  with name 'slipway' 'slip' 'chock' 'rollers' 'stone',
       description "Worn stone, greased for a thousand launches. The chock
           that holds the ship is down under its hull, worked from the deck
           by a lever.";

! --- 7. Inside the Sluice ------------------------------------------------------

Room inside_sluice "Inside the Sluice"
  with description "Dim. Rollers run up into the mountain as far as you can
           see, and far above, the chute is jammed: the dead are piling up in
           cold storage at the summit, waiting for somebody to fix it.
           Somebody is not coming. A lectern stands by the rollers. The way
           out is east.",
       e_to sluice_mouth,
       out_to sluice_mouth;

Feature -> rollers "rollers"
  with name 'rollers' 'roller' 'chute' 'jam' 'summit' 'storage',
       description "They run up into the dark. A long way up, something gilded
           is wedged across them.",
  has  pluralname;

Feature -> lectern "lectern"
  with name 'lectern' 'stand',
       description "A plain wooden lectern, for the register.";

! N1. Its last entry names Councillor Sutt and the Floor Price: you will meet
! her plaque at the intake, in Part Five.
LostName -> launch_register "launch register"
  with name 'launch' 'register' 'ledger' 'entries' 'entry',
       task TASK_REGISTER,
       reading "Councillor Amberline Sutt",
       initial "The launch register lies open on the lectern.",
       description "THE LAUNCH REGISTER OF THE SLUICE. Ship after ship, name
           after name, all in the same hand. The last entry reads: ~the
           Floor Price, for Councillor Amberline Sutt.~ After it, nothing:
           that was the last ship before the jam.";


! ============================================================================
!  7. PART TWO: THE SANDBAR  --  mid-river (rooms 8-9)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  The ship runs aground on its own. Sandwich is at the Wreck first, and his
!  front-run rule decides P2. The sweep (P3) and the grapnel (P4) are here,
!  and so is N2, the Expedition's nameboard.
! ============================================================================

! --- 8. The Sandbar -------------------------------------------------------------

Room the_sandbar "The Sandbar"
  with landing SANDBAR,
       riverside true,
       description "A long pale bar in mid-river, where the funeral ship has
           run aground: the way, by the look of it, they all do. The
           Verdance goes by on both sides. To the east, a wrecked boat lies
           on its side at the end of the bar. The ship is back in.",
       in_to main_deck,
       e_to wreck;

! Sandwich's scull, beached here until he leaves (section 11).
Object -> beached_scull "gilded scull"
  with name 'gilded' 'racing' 'scull' 'slippage' 'oars',
       description "A narrow gilded racing scull, pulled up on the sand. Its
           name is painted on the bow: SLIPPAGE.",
       describe [;
           "^A narrow gilded racing scull is pulled up on the sand.";
       ],
       before [;
         Take, Push, Pull, Enter: "It's Sandwich's. He'd be there first.";
       ],
  has  static;

! --- 9. The Expedition Wreck ------------------------------------------------------

! The room's `before` is where the front-run rule lives: a room's `before`
! runs ahead of the object's own, so the room can step in on the first TAKE
! of any of the four salvage items while Sandwich is here. SandwichGrab is
! in section 11.
Room wreck "The Expedition Wreck"
  with landing SANDBAR,
       riverside true,
       description "The Step Three lies on her side at the end of the bar,
           her planks gone grey. She was the Verdance Expedition's boat, and
           painted along her side is the Expedition's roadmap: STEP 1: BOAT.
           STEP 2: RIVER. STEP 3: CROSS. Her thwart board has come loose and
           lies in the sand. The bar runs back west.",
       w_to the_sandbar,
       before [;
         Take: if (sandwich in self) return SandwichGrab(noun);
         Remove: if (sandwich in self && noun in sandwich)
                     "He's already out of reach, smiling. Latency.";
       ];

Feature -> step_three "Step Three"
  with name 'step' 'three' 'wreck' 'wrecked' 'boat' 'roadmap' 'planks',
       description "Her roadmap ended at STEP 3: CROSS. She got as far as a
           sandbar.";

! The sweep: the long steering oar for the stern socket (P3).
Object -> sweep "sweep"
  with name 'sweep' 'oar' 'steering' 'long',
       initial "A long steering sweep lies along the wreck's thwarts.",
       ! Once shipped, the Stern's description mentions it; `describe`
       ! returning true stops the library listing it a second time.
       describe [; if (sweep_shipped) rtrue; rfalse; ],
       description "A long steering oar. Its foot is worn round, to sit in a
           socket.",
       before [;
         Take: if (sweep_shipped) "It's shipped in the socket. Leave it
             there.";
       ];

! The grapnel: thrown astern it kedges you off the bar (P4); thrown at the
! chain in passage 3's window it saves the ship (P7). Every way of throwing
! or dropping it goes to one routine, GrapnelThrown (section 9).
Object -> grapnel "grapnel"
  with name 'grapnel' 'grapple' 'hook' 'anchor' 'tines',
       initial "A grapnel on a coil of line lies half buried in the sand.",
       describe [;
           if (grapnel_hooked) "^The grapnel line runs up from the rail to the
               chain overhead, bar-taut.";
           rfalse;
       ],
       description [;
           if (grapnel_hooked) "Bitten into the chain overhead. The whole ship
               hangs from it.";
           "A four-tined iron grapnel on a long coil of line.";
       ],
       before [;
         Drop: return GrapnelThrown(0);
         ThrowAt: return GrapnelThrown(second);
         Take: if (grapnel_hooked) "It's holding the whole ship. UNHOOK it
             when you're ready to go.";
         Pull, Unhook: if (grapnel_hooked) return UnhookGrapnel();
       ];

! The strongbox: gilt, so Sandwich always wants it. If he leaves without it
! (the coat route), you can take it and open it.
Object -> strongbox "strongbox"
  with name 'strongbox' 'treasury' 'gilt' 'stencilled',
       initial "A gilded strongbox, stencilled EXPEDITION TREASURY, sits in
           the wreck's bilge.",
       description "A gilded strongbox stencilled EXPEDITION TREASURY.",
  has  container openable;

Object safu_note "note" strongbox
  with name 'note' 'safu' 'funds',
       description "FUNDS ARE SAFU.";

! N2: five names scratched into the thwart board. Only this object answers
! to NAME and NAMES, so EXAMINE NAMES never asks "which do you mean?".
LostName -> nameboard "nameboard"
  with name 'nameboard' 'thwart' 'board' 'names' 'name' 'scratched',
       task TASK_NAMEBOARD,
       reading "Nonie Pask",
       description "The Step Three's thwart board. Five names are scratched
           into it with a knife point, one under another: DELL ARKWRIGHT,
           NONIE PASK, TOMAS VERE, ILSA GRUND, BEN OKORO. Under them, in the
           same hand: GOT THIS FAR.";


! ============================================================================
!  8. PART THREE: THE OLD-SIDE BANK  --  the taboo is broken (rooms 10-14)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  Lucan leads you up the bank by his favourite verse, to the lifebelt (P6).
!  The old side is seen only from outside, and never explained. Mrs.
!  Thrumble answers the bell at the pier. N3, Lucan's songbook, reaches you
!  through her.
! ============================================================================

! --- 10. The Stone Pier -------------------------------------------------------

! Leaving the pier sends Mrs. Thrumble below. The room's `before` catches
! every Go: noun.door_dir is the exit property for that direction (n_to,
! in_to...), so self.(noun.door_dir) is non-zero exactly when there is an
! exit that way -- she only leaves if you really do.
Room stone_pier "The Stone Pier"
  with landing PIER,
       riverside true,
       description "Old fitted stone, the joints so tight a knife wouldn't go
           in, older than anything on the Mingus side. The funeral ship is
           made fast to an iron ring set in the pier. Set into the bank at the
           pier's head is an iron hatch with a barred window, a bell on a
           bracket, and a card. The ship is back in; the bank rises to the
           north.",
       in_to main_deck,
       n_to mine_heads,
       before [;
         Go:
           if (thrumble in self && self.(noun.door_dir) ~= 0) {
               remove thrumble;
               print "The shutter closes.^^";
           }
       ];

Feature -> iron_ring "iron ring"
  with name 'iron' 'ring',
       description "A plain iron ring, worn bright on the inside by ropes. The
           ship's mooring rope is made fast to it.";

Feature -> lost_hatch "iron hatch"
  with name 'hatch' 'window' 'barred' 'bars' 'shutter' 'counter',
       description [;
           if (thrumble in stone_pier) "Behind the bars, Mrs. Thrumble is
               writing in a ledger. She does not look up.";
           "An iron hatch in the bank, with a barred window and a shutter
           behind the bars. The shutter is down.";
       ],
       before [;
         Open, Enter: "It isn't that kind of hatch. There's a bell.";
       ];

Feature -> lost_card "card"
  with name 'card' 'sign',
       description "LOST PROPERTY. RING ONCE. DOWNSTREAM COUNTER.";

! RING BELL brings Mrs. Thrumble (ThrumbleRing, section 11).
Object -> bell "bell"
  with name 'bell' 'bracket',
       description "A small brass bell on a bracket by the hatch.",
       before [;
         Ring, Push, Pull: return ThrumbleRing();
       ],
  has  static scenery;

! The mooring rope: on deck and on the pier while the ship is made fast
! here. UNTIE it to cast off (passage 3). Its parse_name answers to SHIP as
! well, but only on the Main Deck (on the pier, SHIP means the ship itself),
! so UNTIE SHIP works in both places.
Object mooring_rope "mooring rope"
  with parse_name [ n w;
           for (::) {
               w = NextWord();
               if (w == 'mooring' or 'rope' or 'line' or 'painter') { n++; continue; }
               if (w == 'ship' && location == main_deck) { n++; continue; }
               return n;
           }
       ],
       found_in [;
           return (ship_at == PIER && location == main_deck or stone_pier);
       ],
       description "The ship's mooring rope, made fast to the iron ring on
           the pier.",
       before [;
         Untie, Take, Pull: return CastOffPier();
       ],
  has  static scenery;

! The cast-off for the Stone Pier, with its two gates, in order. Each
! refusal says what is missing. A refused cast-off still passes a turn.
[ CastOffPier;
    if (lucan_state ~= DEAD) "Lucan, from the bank: ~Not yet! Twenty years
        I've sung this bank!~";
    if (~~IndirectlyContains(player, lifebelt)) "Not without something that
        floats. Every song about this river ends in white water.";
    if (location == stone_pier) {
        if (thrumble in stone_pier) {
            remove thrumble;
            print "The shutter closes.^^";
        }
        PlayerTo(main_deck, 1);
        print "You slip the rope off the iron ring and step aboard as the
            current takes the ship.";
    }
    else print "You cast off. The rope runs out through the iron ring and
        falls in the water.";
    print " The old side slides away behind you, green and warm and quiet.^";
    CastOff(3);
    rtrue;
];

! --- 11. The Mine Heads -----------------------------------------------------------

! The signpost for Lucan's death is here, before it happens: the bright,
! stone-ringed patches, and (EXAMINE PATCHES) the warm air leaking round
! the stones.
Room mine_heads "The Mine Heads"
  with landing PIER,
       description "Timber headframes stand over capped shafts, grey with
           age. Iron grates in the grass breathe warm air, and low chimney
           vents give off a steady glow that never flickers. Here and there
           the grass is a brighter green, smooth as baize, each patch ringed
           with small white stones. The pier is back south; the bank rises
           north, and to the east a roofline shows above the grass.",
       s_to stone_pier,
       n_to hollow_green,
       e_to buried_hall;

Feature -> headframes "headframes"
  with name 'timber' 'headframes' 'headframe' 'shafts' 'shaft' 'capped',
       description "Old timber, older than the Ascendancy. The shafts under
           them are capped with iron. Something down there is warm.",
       before [;
         Enter, Climb: "You don't. It isn't that you can't.";
       ],
  has  pluralname;

Feature -> grates "grates"
  with name 'iron' 'grates' 'grate' 'vents' 'vent' 'chimney' 'chimneys'
           'glow',
       description "The grates breathe warm air. The chimney vents glow,
           steadily, the way nothing on the Mingus side ever has. It never
           flickers.",
       before [;
         Enter, Open: "You don't. It isn't that you can't.";
       ],
  has  pluralname;

! PATCH and PATCHES are different words even in z5 (both under nine
! letters), so one object can answer to both. Stepping on the patches is
! never deadly to you: before Lucan's death you are steered off them, and
! after it you remember.
Object -> mine_patches "bright patches"
  with name 'patch' 'patches' 'bright' 'baize' 'turf' 'white' 'stones'
           'stone' 'ring',
       description "Patches of turf a brighter green than the rest, smooth as
           baize, each one ringed with small white stones. Warm air leaks
           out round the stones, as if the grass were a lid.",
       before [;
         Enter: return StepOnPatch();
         Take, Pull, Dig: "The turf is tight and smooth, and warm under your
             hand. You leave it.";
       ],
  has  static scenery pluralname;

! STEP ON PATCH, ENTER PATCH, STAND ON PATCH: one answer for both rooms.
[ StepOnPatch;
    if (lucan_state == DEAD) "You remember Lucan. You don't.";
    if (lucan in location) "Lucan is standing on the best one, singing.";
    "Something about the bright turf makes you keep to the plain grass.";
];

! --- 12. Outside the Half-Buried Hall --------------------------------------------

! The old side is never entered. IN, DOWN, ENTER and OPEN DOOR all get the
! same line; the exits are routines that print it and stay put.
Room buried_hall "Outside the Half-Buried Hall"
  with landing PIER,
       description "A roofline at the height of the grass, and below it a row
           of windows at ground level, with warm light in them. A door stands
           half below the turf, with steps going down to it. Nobody comes to
           the windows. The mine heads are back west.",
       w_to mine_heads,
       in_to [; "You don't. It isn't that you can't."; ],
       d_to  [; "You don't. It isn't that you can't."; ];

Feature -> hall_door "door"
  with name 'door' 'steps' 'hall' 'roofline' 'roof' 'windows' 'window'
           'light',
       description "The door is half below the turf, and the steps go down to
           it. The light in the windows is warm and steady.",
       before [;
         Enter, Open, Push, Climb: "You don't. It isn't that you can't.";
       ];

! --- 13. The Hollow Green -----------------------------------------------------------

! P6 lives here, and so does Lucan's death (section 11). Before it: the
! patches and the lifebelt. After it: the hole and his hat. DOWN is always
! refused, with a different line once he is gone.
Room hollow_green "The Hollow Green"
  with landing PIER,
       riverside true,
       description [;
           print "Here the green bank goes down to the water, the way the song
               says. There are more of the bright patches, ringed with white
               stones, all across the hollow";
           if (lucan_state == DEAD) print ", and among them is a dark hole in
               the bright turf, ringed with white stones, breathing warm air.
               Lucan's hat lies at its edge";
           print ".";
           if (lifebelt in hollow_green) print " A white lifebelt hangs on a
               post at the water's edge.";
           else print " A bare post stands at the water's edge.";
           "^^The mine heads are back south. To the north, the bank runs on to
           an old fence.";
       ],
       s_to mine_heads,
       n_to old_fence,
       d_to [;
           if (lucan_state == DEAD) "You remember Lucan. You don't.";
           "The bank goes down to the water here, and the water goes on
           without you.";
       ];

Object -> green_patches "bright patches"
  with name 'patch' 'patches' 'bright' 'baize' 'turf' 'green' 'white'
           'stones' 'stone',
       description "Bright turf, smooth as a stage, ringed with small white
           stones. Warm air leaks round the stones.",
       before [;
         Enter: return StepOnPatch();
         Take, Pull, Dig: "The turf is tight and smooth, and warm under your
             hand. You leave it.";
       ],
  has  static scenery pluralname;

Feature -> lifebelt_post "post"
  with name 'post' 'wooden',
       description "A weathered post at the water's edge.";

! P6: the lifebelt. Points come with taking it (in `after`, so the library
! has already moved it to you). It is clothing, so WEAR LIFEBELT works -- and
! wearing it is what P10 checks.
! Its parse_name lets RING mean the lifebelt (Lucan calls it "a ring on a
! post"), except at the Stone Pier, where RING means the iron ring.
Object -> lifebelt "lifebelt"
  with parse_name [ n w;
           for (::) {
               w = NextWord();
               if (w == 'lifebelt' or 'life' or 'belt' or 'cork' or 'white'
                   or 'buoy' or 'lifebuoy') { n++; continue; }
               if (w == 'ring' && location ~= stone_pier) { n++; continue; }
               return n;
           }
       ],
       describe [; rtrue; ],    ! the room description mentions it
       description "A ring of cork, painted white, older than anything on
           the Mingus side. It still floats; you can tell by the weight.",
       after [;
         Take:
           Achieved(TASK_LIFEBELT);
           "You lift the lifebelt off its post. Cork, painted white. The song
           was right about this.";
       ],
  has  clothing;

! After Lucan's death, LucanDies (section 11) moves these two here.
Object lucan_hole "hole"
  with name 'dark' 'hole' 'trap' 'warm' 'air',
       description "A dark hole in the bright turf, ringed with white stones,
           breathing warm air. You can't see the bottom.",
       before [;
         Enter, Search, Climb: "You remember Lucan. You don't.";
       ],
  has  static scenery;

Object lucan_hat "Lucan's hat"
  with name 'hat' 'velvet' 'band' 'lucan^s',
       description "Velvet band. A Thursday hat.",
       before [;
         Take, Pull, Wear: "You leave it where he can find it.";
       ],
  has  static scenery;

! --- 14. The Old Fence ---------------------------------------------------------------

! A glimpse, and nothing more: there is nothing to take, and nothing is
! explained.
Room old_fence "The Old Fence"
  with landing PIER,
       description "A long, low, grass-covered hill behind an old fence. A
           gate that hasn't opened in years. You could walk past it a hundred
           times. The hollow green is back south.",
       s_to hollow_green;

Feature -> fence "old fence"
  with name 'old' 'fence' 'gate' 'hill' 'grass' 'grassy' 'long' 'low',
       description "From the road it is nothing. From here it is a long, low
           hill, and a fence, and a gate that hasn't opened in years.",
       before [;
         Climb, Open, Enter, Push, Unlock, JumpOver:
           "Not today. Something about that hill makes your hands want to
           hold on to the fence.";
       ];


! ============================================================================
!  9. PART FOUR: THE GILDED SPAN  --  mid-river (rooms 15-16)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  Passage 3's window is THROW GRAPNEL AT CHAIN (P7); miss it and the ship
!  dies under the broken end. Up the chain: the diver's tag (N4) and the
!  civic key in its velvet box (P8). You can't leave without the key.
! ============================================================================

! --- The grapnel's routines (it is used in Part Two as well) -----------------

! GrapnelThrown(target): THROW GRAPNEL, THROW GRAPNEL AT/INTO <target> and
! DROP GRAPNEL all end up here. target 0 means "no target". Throwing it
! astern while aground is the kedge (P4); at the chain, P7; anything else is
! refused, so it can never be lost.
[ GrapnelThrown target;
    if (target == 0 or verdance) {
        if (location == stern && ship_at == SANDBAR) return Kedge();
        "You'd lose it.";
    }
    if (target == chain) return ThrowAtChain();
    "You'd lose it.";
];

! P4, part one. The gate: you can't kedge off without a way to steer.
[ Kedge;
    if (~~sweep_shipped) "You'd be off the bar and into the river with no way
        to steer. Even a funeral ship deserves better.";
    remove grapnel;
    kedge_set = true;
    MoveFloatingObjects();          ! the kedge line appears at once
    "You swing the grapnel and throw it out over the stern. It splashes down
    in the deep water, sinks, and bites into the riverbed astern. The line
    comes taut.";
];

! P4, part two: PULL LINE is the cast-off for passage 2.
[ PullKedge;
    kedge_set = false;
    move grapnel to player;
    Achieved(TASK_KEDGE);
    print "You haul on the line, hand over hand. For a long moment nothing
        happens. Then the ship comes grinding off the bar stern first, into
        deep water, and swings, and the current has it. You bring the grapnel
        in, dripping.^";
    CastOff(2);
    rtrue;
];

! P7: the window. ThrowAtChain is only reachable where the chain is in scope:
! the Prow during passage 3, or once the ship hangs from it.
[ ThrowAtChain;
    if (location ~= prow) "You're already up here with it.";
    if (grapnel_hooked) "It's already holding the chain.";
    if (drift < 3) "The chain is still well ahead.";
    move grapnel to prow;
    grapnel_hooked = true;
    ShipTo(SPAN);
    Achieved(TASK_CHAIN);
    "You swing the grapnel twice and let it go. The grapnel goes up, over, and
    bites. The line snaps taut, the ship swings, and hangs, shuddering, under
    the broken end. The current goes on without you.";
];

! The cast-off for the Span, with its gate: everything downstream was built
! by Vaunt, and the key opens anything Vaunt built.
[ UnhookGrapnel;
    if (~~IndirectlyContains(player, civic_key)) "Up there on the girder, the
        velvet box says VAUNT CIVIC KEY -- OPENS ANYTHING VAUNT BUILT.
        Everything downstream was built by Vaunt. You're not leaving it.";
    grapnel_hooked = false;
    move grapnel to player;
    print "You work the grapnel free. The ship drops away from the chain, the
        current takes it, and the broken end slides away overhead. You coil
        the line and keep the grapnel.^";
    CastOff(4);
    rtrue;
];

! The chain: at the Prow (during passage 3 and while held) and at the Broken
! End. CLIMB CHAIN goes up from the Prow; CLIMB (DOWN) CHAIN goes down from
! the Broken End. HOOK CHAIN WITH GRAPNEL is PullWith (section 15) and means
! the same as THROW GRAPNEL AT CHAIN.
Object chain "chain"
  with name 'chain' 'shackle' 'links',
       found_in [;
           if (location == broken_end) rtrue;
           if (location ~= prow) rfalse;
           return (ship_at == SPAN || (ship_at == ADRIFT && passage == 3));
       ],
       description [;
           if (location == broken_end) "The chain hangs from the last girder's
               top shackle down to the prow of your ship.";
           if (ship_at == SPAN) "Your grapnel is bitten into it, and the ship
               hangs from it. You could climb it.";
           "A heavy chain hanging from the broken end of the span, down toward
           the water. If anything on this river could hold a ship, that
           could.";
       ],
       before [;
         Climb:
           if (location == broken_end) <<Go d_obj>>;
           if (ship_at == SPAN) <<Go u_obj>>;
           "It's out over the water, ahead. You'd need to hook it first.";
         PullWith:
           if (second == grapnel) return GrapnelThrown(self);
         Take, Pull: "It's a span's worth of chain. It isn't coming with you.";
       ],
  has  static scenery;

! --- 15. The Broken End ---------------------------------------------------------

! The lore's shared nightmare, written straight. EAST, OUT (WALK OUT) and
! GET ON GIRDER are river attempts; the girder's own line is the warning.
Room broken_end "The Broken End"
  with landing SPAN,
       riverside true,
       river_line "The planks tilt under your first step, very slightly,
           toward the water. You step back.",
       description "The end of Lord Vaunt's gilded suspension span. Behind
           you, VAUNT is written on both towers in letters taller than a
           house. In front of you the planks tilt very slightly toward the
           water, and the last girder hangs out over the current like a
           sentence nobody could finish. A chain hangs from its top shackle
           down to your ship. Out on the girder sits a small velvet box. The
           span runs back west.",
       w_to span_deck,
       d_to prow,
       e_to   [; return RiverAttempt(self.river_line); ],
       out_to [; return RiverAttempt(self.river_line); ];

! The girder: GET ON / STAND ON / ENTER it is a river attempt.
Feature -> girder "last girder"
  with name 'last' 'girder' 'planks' 'plank' 'towers' 'tower' 'span',
       description "It hangs out over the current like a sentence nobody could
           finish. The velvet box is on it, out of reach.",
       before [;
         Enter, Climb: return RiverAttempt(broken_end.river_line);
       ];

! N4: the only applicant there ever was.
LostName -> diver_tag "diver's tag"
  with name 'diver^s' 'tag' 'divers' 'applicant' 'brass',
       task TASK_TAG,
       reading "Applicant No. 1",
       initial "A brass tag hangs from the chain's top shackle.",
       description "VERDANCE FOUNDATION DIVERS -- APPLICANT No. 1. Stamped
           underneath: M. QUILLAN. There was never an applicant No. 2.";

! P8: the velvet box, out on the girder. TAKE is refused (not a river
! attempt, just a refusal). PULL / HOOK / GET BOX WITH PIKE reaches it.
Object -> velvet_box "velvet box"
  with name 'velvet' 'box' 'ribbon' 'ceremonial',
       describe [; rtrue; ],    ! the room description mentions it
       description [;
           if (self has open) "The ceremonial box for ~Phase 2: Far Bank~,
               open and empty on the planks.";
           "A ceremonial velvet box with a ribbon, labelled in gilt: PHASE 2:
           FAR BANK. VAUNT CIVIC KEY -- OPENS ANYTHING VAUNT BUILT.";
       ],
       before [;
         Take, Pull, Push:
           if (self has open) "It's empty. Leave it.";
           "Out on the girder. The planks between you tilt.";
         PullWith:
           if (self has open) "It's empty. Leave it.";
           if (second ~= pike) "It doesn't reach. You'd need something
               long.";
           give self open;
           move civic_key to player;
           Achieved(TASK_KEY);
           "You reach the pike out over the girder, catch the box by its
           ribbon, and ease it home. It falls open: on the velvet lies a
           gilded key, stamped VAUNT CIVIC KEY. You take it.";
       ],
  has  static;

Object civic_key "Vaunt civic key"
  with name 'vaunt' 'civic' 'key' 'gilded',
       description "A gilded key stamped VAUNT CIVIC KEY. OPENS ANYTHING VAUNT
           BUILT, says the box it came from.",
       before [;
         Take: if (self in stop_switch) "It stays in the switch. Vaunt's last
             civic act.";
       ];

! --- 16. The Span Deck ---------------------------------------------------------

Room span_deck "The Span Deck"
  with landing SPAN,
       description "The span's deck, gilded and empty. A tollbooth stands with
           its window down. On a post is the roadmap plaque, and on the
           tollbooth is a recruiting poster. Back toward the Mingus bank the
           span is closed by a chained gate: BRIDGE CLOSED FOR PHASE 2. The
           broken end is east.",
       e_to broken_end,
       w_to "The gate is chained: BRIDGE CLOSED FOR PHASE 2.";

Feature -> tollbooth "tollbooth"
  with name 'tollbooth' 'toll' 'booth',
       description "TOLL: ONE $ALT. EXACT CHANGE. Nobody has ever paid it.";

Feature -> roadmap_plaque "roadmap plaque"
  with name 'roadmap' 'plaque' 'post',
       description "PHASE 1: BRIDGE. PHASE 2: FAR BANK.";

Feature -> poster "poster"
  with name 'poster' 'recruiting' 'divers' 'foundation',
       description "THE VERDANCE FOUNDATION DIVERS. JOIN THE FIRST DEEP-WATER
           TEAM ON THE RIVER! Generous token allocation. Vesting schedule
           TBD.";

Feature -> span_gate "chained gate"
  with name 'chained' 'gate' 'chains',
       description "BRIDGE CLOSED FOR PHASE 2.",
       before [;
         Open, Unlock, Climb: "It's chained, and Phase 2 never came.";
       ];


! ============================================================================
!  10. PART FIVE: THE INTAKE  --  the Mingus bank (rooms 17-19)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  The current pins the ship to the intake screen. Sandwich is pinned there
!  too, holding N5. Vaunt's key in Vaunt's switch stops the pump (P9); the
!  next turn you are aboard, the ship comes off the screen into the Race.
! ============================================================================

! --- 17. The Intake Screen ---------------------------------------------------

Room intake_screen "The Intake Screen"
  with landing INTAKE,
       riverside true,
       description [;
           print "A catwalk along the vast gilded grating of PUMPING STATION
               No. 2 -- INTAKE. It pumps the Verdance up the mountain, the
               one thing in the Ascendancy that goes uphill";
           if (pump_stopped) print ". It is still now, and the water goes
               through the grating slowly";
           else print ", and the whole screen hums with it";
           ". By the catwalk rail is a stop switch. Far off along the Mingus
           bank the verdlings stand in silent rows, watching. Your ship is
           back in; a shed stands to the west, and to the north a road comes
           down to the water.";
       ],
       in_to main_deck,
       w_to divers_shed,
       n_to drowned_road;

Feature -> grating "grating"
  with name 'gilded' 'grating' 'screen' 'intake' 'pump' 'station',
       description [;
           if (pump_stopped) "Still. For the first time in its life, the
               Ascendancy has stopped drinking the river.";
           "PUMPING STATION No. 2 -- INTAKE. It is drinking the Verdance and
           sending it up the mountain.";
       ];

! P9. PUT KEY IN SWITCH sends the switch a ##Receive (it is not a container,
! but `before` runs before the library would refuse). UNLOCK SWITCH WITH KEY
! and TURN SWITCH WITH KEY are ##Unlock. It is `transparent`, so the key
! is still visible once it is in.
Object -> stop_switch "stop switch"
  with name 'stop' 'switch' 'emergency' 'keyhole',
       description [;
           if (pump_stopped) "EMERGENCY STOP -- VAUNT CIVIC KEY ONLY. The
               civic key is in it, turned.";
           "EMERGENCY STOP -- VAUNT CIVIC KEY ONLY. There is a keyhole.";
       ],
       before [;
         Receive, Unlock:
           if ((action == ##Receive && noun ~= civic_key) ||
               (action == ##Unlock && second ~= civic_key))
               "It takes a Vaunt civic key, and only that.";
           return StopPump();
         Push, Pull, Turn, SwitchOff:
           if (pump_stopped) "It's done.";
           "EMERGENCY STOP -- VAUNT CIVIC KEY ONLY. The keyhole waits.";
       ],
  has  static scenery transparent;

[ StopPump;
    if (pump_stopped) "It's done.";
    pump_stopped = true;
    move civic_key to stop_switch;
    Achieved(TASK_PUMP);
    print "You put the civic key in the switch and turn it. The pump runs down
        with a sound like the mountain exhaling. For the first time in its
        life, the Ascendancy stops drinking the river.^";
    SandwichPaysUp();
    rtrue;
];

! N5: Sandwich holds it until the pump stops (section 11).
LostName sutt_plaque "funeral plaque"
  with name 'funeral' 'plaque' 'sutt' 'amberline' 'councillor' 'seed'
           'words',
       task TASK_PLAQUE,
       reading "Amberline Sutt",
       description "A funeral plaque: AMBERLINE SUTT -- COUNCILLOR, and twelve
           seed words, very small. It is the Floor Price's plaque, from the
           launch register.",
       before [;
         Take, Remove:
           if (self in sandwich) "~Unpin me first, chief. Then we'll talk.~";
       ];

! --- 18. The Divers' Shed ------------------------------------------------------

Room divers_shed "The Divers' Shed"
  with landing INTAKE,
       description "The Verdance Foundation Divers' recruiting shed, never
           used. A sign-up sheet is pinned to the wall, and a diving helmet
           sits on a shelf. The catwalk is back east.",
       e_to intake_screen;

Feature -> signup_sheet "sign-up sheet"
  with name 'sign-up' 'signup' 'sheet' 'wall',
       description "The sign-up sheet has room for a hundred divers. The first
           line has been torn off. The rest are blank.";

Feature -> helmet "diving helmet"
  with name 'diving' 'helmet' 'shelf',
       description "It has never been wet.";

! --- 19. The Drowned Road ------------------------------------------------------

! Written straight: no jokes here. DOWN, EAST and WALK DOWN ROAD are river
! attempts, with the road's own warning line.
Room drowned_road "The Drowned Road"
  with landing INTAKE,
       riverside true,
       river_line "You take one step down the road toward the water, and the
           water seems to take one step toward you. You step back.",
       description "A road comes down out of the Ascendancy and runs straight
           into the Verdance. Its centre line goes on under the water, a
           little blurred, for as far as you can see into the green. At the
           top of the road a car stands with its engine ticking and its
           headlights on, pointed down the road. The driver's door is open.
           There is nobody in it and nobody near it, and the road is very
           clearly meant to be driven. The intake is back south.",
       s_to intake_screen,
       d_to [; return RiverAttempt(self.river_line); ],
       e_to [; return RiverAttempt(self.river_line); ];

Feature -> the_road "road"
  with name 'road' 'centre' 'center' 'line' 'tarmac',
       description "The centre line goes on under the water, a little
           blurred, as far as you can see.",
       before [;
         Enter: return RiverAttempt(drowned_road.river_line);
       ];

Feature -> the_car "car"
  with name 'car' 'engine' 'headlights' 'door' 'driver^s',
       description "The engine is ticking. The headlights are on. The driver's
           door is open. Nobody is in it.",
       before [;
         Enter, Drive, Open, Close: "You don't. You know exactly where it
             goes.";
       ];

! The Verdance itself: in every riverside room. ENTER RIVER / JUMP IN RIVER /
! SWIM IN RIVER are river attempts, with the room's own line.
Object verdance "Verdance"
  with name 'verdance' 'river' 'water' 'current' 'stream',
       found_in [; return location.riverside; ],
       description "Broad and green and fast, older and stranger than the
           Ascendancy. It goes by quick and quiet.",
       before [;
         Enter, JumpIn, Swim: return RiverAttempt(location.river_line);
         Drink: "It tastes of a long way down.";
         Take: "It runs through your fingers, on its way somewhere.";
       ],
  has  scenery proper;


! ============================================================================
!  11. NPCS  --  Lucan (the showcase), Sandwich, Mrs. Thrumble
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  Three patterns:
!    LUCAN     a companion: follows, leads, talks, reacts, dies on a timer
!    SANDWICH  an antagonist on a fixed, learnable schedule
!    THRUMBLE  a stationary service NPC who answers a bell
!  Each NPC's end-of-turn behaviour is ONE routine (LucanTick, SandwichTick),
!  and the dispatcher in section 12 calls them in a fixed order. Nothing an
!  NPC does at the end of a turn happens anywhere else.
! ============================================================================

! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  LUCAN FAIRWEATHER, the Thursday tenor
! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  LESSON: the state machine. One Global, lucan_state, says what he is doing:
!
!    HIDDEN     not in play (stowed away in the hold)
!       | passage 1, drift 2: he climbs out          (LucanTick)
!    FOLLOWING  walks after you, on the ship and at the landing it is at
!       | the end of the turn the ship lands at the Stone Pier
!    GUIDING    goes ahead of you: Stone Pier -> Mine Heads -> Hollow Green
!       | you and he are both in the Hollow Green
!    ARRIVED    lucan_timer counts: 1 = the verse, 2 = the death
!       | timer 2, whatever you do
!    DEAD       removed from play; his hat and the hole remain
!
!  Rule of thumb: the STATE decides what he does at the end of a turn
!  (LucanTick); his `life` and `before` decide how he reacts to what YOU do.
!  Copy this split for any NPC of your own.
! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

! He starts off-stage: an object defined with no `->` and no location is in
! no room at all. LucanTick moves him in when his moment comes.
Person lucan "Lucan Fairweather"
  with name 'lucan' 'fairweather' 'tenor' 'large' 'man' 'singer'
           'waistcoat',
       ! LESSON: `describe` is the line the room description prints for
       ! him -- one per state, so what you see matches what he is doing.
       describe [;
           switch (lucan_state) {
             FOLLOWING: "^Lucan Fairweather is here, humming, his hat pushed
                 back.";
             GUIDING:   "^Lucan Fairweather is here, impatient to go on.";
             ARRIVED:   "^Lucan Fairweather stands at the edge of the bright
                 green, singing to the water.";
           }
       ],
       description "A large, kind man in a velvet waistcoat and a hat with a
           velvet band, magnificently theatrical even when standing still.
           He has sung about the old side for twenty years.",
       ! LESSON: the greeting. HELLO, HI, SAY HELLO, SAY HELLO TO LUCAN and
       ! GREET LUCAN all come here (HelloSub, section 15).
       greeting "Lucan sweeps a bow that nearly takes him over the rail.
           ~Your servant, chief.~",
       ! LESSON: physical actions on an NPC go through `before`, like any
       ! object. These are the ways a player might try to save him -- and
       ! why none of them can: the scene is scripted on purpose.
       before [;
         Take, Pull, Warn:
           "He waves you off mid-verse.";
         Push:
           "He doesn't budge. A tenor is built on a firm foundation.";
       ],
       ! LESSON: `life` handles the conversational and social actions.
       ! ASK and TELL share one topic table (LucanTopic); ANSWER (SAY X TO
       ! LUCAN) uses it too. GIVE and SHOW share one reaction table.
       life [;
         Ask, Tell, Answer:
           return LucanTopic();
         Give, Show:
           return LucanReacts(noun);
         Kiss:
           "He offers a cheek, theatrically. ~Thursdays only.~";
         Attack:
           "You'd sooner hit the river. He's the only friend you have down
           here.";
       ],
       ! LUCAN, SING (an order) gets the fallback line.
       orders [;
           "Lucan considers this, then sings a line about something else
           entirely.";
       ],
  has  male proper;

! LESSON: conversation -- a topic table, read top to bottom. AskedAbout
! (below) is true when any of its words is in the topic the player typed
! ("ask lucan about the old side"). The first row that matches answers; the
! last line is the FALLBACK, for anything the table doesn't know. One
! exception comes first: TELLING him about the patches is a warning, and
! he waves it off.
[ LucanTopic;
    if (action == ##Tell && AskedAbout('patches', 'patch', 'turf', 'green',
        'stones')) "He waves you off mid-verse.";
    if (AskedAbout('old', 'side', 'across', 'far', 'bank'))
        "~Twenty years I've sung it. Lamplight and low ceilings in one song, a
        green field over a sleeping city in the next. Tonight I find out
        which.~";
    if (AskedAbout('song', 'songs', 'verse', 'poets'))
        "~~Where the green bank goes down to the water, there's a door that
        was never a door.~ That one. It's always been that one.~";
    if (AskedAbout('sandwich', 'runner', 'boards', 'scull'))
        "~He's always first, that one, and he's never once arrived anywhere.
        Reach for the gilt and he'll have it -- he has never wanted anything
        plain in his life.~";
    if (AskedAbout('verdlings', 'verdling'))
        "~They bowed to you. To you. I'd have given my voice for that.~";
    if (AskedAbout('river', 'verdance', 'current', 'water'))
        "~No one comes back. That's the line everybody knows. Nobody knows the
        next line, because nobody came back to write it.~";
    if (AskedAbout('lantern'))
        "~Beckoning or warning. I sing it both ways, alternate Thursdays.~";
    if (AskedAbout('ascendancy', 'mingus', 'mountain', 'ballroom'))
        "~Thursdays in the ballroom, upside down, for $ALT. I don't miss it. I
        miss the acoustics.~";
    if (AskedAbout('lucan', 'tenor', 'himself', 'you'))
        "~Lucan Fairweather. Tenor. Thursdays. Retired, as of last night.~";
    if (AskedAbout('patches', 'patch', 'turf', 'green', 'stones'))
        "~Like little stages! The song says the green is a floor.~";
    if (AskedAbout('ship', 'funeral', 'plaque'))
        "~It was built for the dead. I'm hiding in it. Draw your own
        conclusions; I have.~";
    if (AskedAbout('lifebelt', 'ring', 'belt'))
        "~Everything that floats is a song waiting to happen.~";
    "Lucan considers this, then sings a line about something else
    entirely.";
];

! AskedAbout(w1 ... w6): true if any of the given words appears anywhere in
! the topic of the current ASK/TELL/ANSWER. The parser leaves the topic's
! first word number in consult_from and its length in consult_words.
[ AskedAbout w1 w2 w3 w4 w5 w6  i w;
    wn = consult_from;
    for (i = 0 : i < consult_words : i++) {
        w = NextWord();
        if (w ~= 0 && (w == w1 or w2 or w3 or w4 or w5 or w6)) rtrue;
    }
    rfalse;
];

! LESSON: reacting to GIVE and SHOW, item by item. He never keeps anything:
! the routine prints and returns true, so the library never moves the item.
[ LucanReacts item;
    if (item == coat) "~Esme's coat! I sang at her wedding. Both weddings.
        Same song.~";
    if (item == pike) "He steps back. ~Not to me, chief. They'd never forgive
        either of us.~";
    if (item ofclass LostName) {
        print "He reads one name aloud, quietly -- ~", (string) item.reading,
            "~ -- and takes his hat off. ~Somebody should sing them.~^";
        rtrue;
    }
    if (item == lifebelt) "~Ha! The song was right about something.~";
    "Lucan admires it politely and gives it back.";
];

! LESSON: the route. Leading needs to know "how far along" a room is, so the
! route is an array and RouteIndex looks a room up in it (-1 = not on it).
Array lucan_route --> stone_pier mine_heads hollow_green;

[ RouteIndex room  i;
    for (i = 0 : i < 3 : i++) if (lucan_route-->i == room) return i;
    return -1;
];

! LucanTick: everything Lucan does at the end of a turn, by state. Called by
! the dispatcher (section 12) after the river and before Sandwich.
[ LucanTick;
    switch (lucan_state) {
      HIDDEN:    LucanStowaway();
      FOLLOWING: LucanFollows();
      GUIDING:   LucanLeads();
      ARRIVED:   LucanArrived();
    }
];

! LESSON: a hidden NPC that announces itself. Passage 1, drift 1: a sneeze
! (a clue). Drift 2: the entrance, worded for where you are standing.
[ LucanStowaway;
    if (passage ~= 1) return;
    if (drift == 1) {
        if (location == hold) print "^From under the medallions, someone
            sneezes.^";
        else print "^From the hold, someone sneezes.^";
        return;
    }
    if (drift ~= 2) return;
    move lucan to location;
    lucan_state = FOLLOWING;
    if (location == hold) print "^The medallions shift, and slide, and a large
        man in a velvet waistcoat climbs out of them, brushing off $ALT.";
    else print "^The hatch bangs open, and a large man in a velvet waistcoat
        climbs up through it, brushing off $ALT.";
    print " ~Lucan Fairweather. Tenor. Thursdays.~ In one breath he explains
        that he has sung about the old side for twenty years, that a Mingan
        on the valley floor gets poked, and that this ship is his only
        chance.^";
];

! LESSON: following. If he isn't in your room, and your room is somewhere
! he can go (the ship, or the landing it is at), he moves there and says so.
! The landing ends this state: the turn the ship reaches the Stone Pier, he
! is over the rail first, and GUIDING begins.
[ LucanFollows;
    if (ship_at == PIER) {
        move lucan to stone_pier;
        lucan_state = GUIDING;
        print "^Lucan is over the rail and onto the pier before the ship has
            stopped moving.^";
        return;
    }
    if (lucan in location) return;
    if (location ofclass ShipRoom || location.landing == ship_at) {
        move lucan to location;
        print "^Lucan follows you, humming.^";
    }
];

! LESSON: leading, with catch-up. The first rule that matches applies:
!  (a) WAIT, THEN GO ON: you are in his room and were already there when the
!      turn began (you didn't just arrive) -- he goes one room on. So when
!      you arrive he waits one turn, and any action at all lets him go.
!  (b) CATCH UP: you are further along the route than he is -- he hurries
!      after you. You can't outrun the script.
!  (c) CALL YOU ON: you are elsewhere on the old-side bank or on the ship.
!  (d) ARRIVAL, checked after the rest: you are both in the Hollow Green.
! "You just arrived" is location ~= last_location: the dispatcher saves
! last_location at the end of every turn.
[ LucanLeads  here mine;
    here = RouteIndex(parent(lucan));
    mine = RouteIndex(location);
    if (lucan in location && location == last_location && here < 2) {
        move lucan to lucan_route-->(here + 1);
        print "^Lucan goes north, singing. ~This way!~^";
    }
    else if (mine > here) {
        move lucan to location;
        print "^Lucan hurries up behind you, out of breath. ~Wait for me!~^";
    }
    else if (lucan notin location &&
             (location.landing == PIER || location ofclass ShipRoom))
        print "^Somewhere ahead, Lucan is singing, and calling for you to keep
            up.^";
    if (lucan in hollow_green && location == hollow_green) {
        lucan_state = ARRIVED;
        lucan_timer = 0;
        print "^~There!~ Lucan points. ~A ring on a post, where the green bank
            goes down to the water. The song never said what for.~^";
    }
];

! LESSON: a scripted, timed event. Once ARRIVED, a timer counts turns, and
! nothing the player does changes it: timer 1 is the verse (and the step
! back, onto the bright green); timer 2 is the death. The text depends on
! whether you are there to see it; the event does not.
[ LucanArrived;
    lucan_timer++;
    if (lucan_timer == 1) {
        if (location == hollow_green) print "^Lucan sings the verse through
            once, softly, for the first time in the place it was written
            about. Then he steps back to take it all in, onto the bright
            green.^";
        return;
    }
    LucanDies();
];

! LESSON: what persists after death. Remove the NPC, then leave his traces
! in the world: the hole and his hat here, his nest in the Hold (the Hold's
! description checks lucan_state), Mrs. Thrumble's line and his songbook
! (ThrumbleRing), Sandwich's question (SandwichTick), and a line in the
! ending (section 13).
[ LucanDies;
    remove lucan;
    lucan_state = DEAD;
    move lucan_hole to hollow_green;
    move lucan_hat to hollow_green;
    if (location == hollow_green) print "^The bright turf gives under him
        without a sound, the way a stage trap opens. For a moment there is a
        hole in the grass, and warm air coming up out of it, and the last
        note of the verse. Then Lucan is gone.^";
    else print "^From somewhere behind you, the song stops in the middle of a
        line.^";
];

! FOLLOW LUCAN works even when he has gone on ahead and is out of sight. A
! scope routine (FollowScope, section 15) puts him in scope for this one
! verb, wherever he is, so the parser can match his name.
[ FollowLucan;
    if (lucan in location) "He's right here, singing at you.";
    if (lucan_state == GUIDING && RouteIndex(location) >= 0 &&
        RouteIndex(parent(lucan)) == RouteIndex(location) + 1) <<Go n_obj>>;
    "You can't see where he went.";
];

! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  SANDWICH, the front-runner
! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  LESSON: an antagonist on a fixed schedule. Every appearance is listed in
!  sandwich_state's constants (section 1), and his interference follows one
!  rule he says out loud, so it is learnable: "Whatever you reach for, I'll
!  have reached for it first." He never takes what you already carry.
! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Person sandwich "Sandwich" gangplank_foot
  with name 'sandwich' 'front-runner' 'frontrunner' 'thin' 'boards'
           'sandwich-boards' 'scull' 'slippage' 'boathook',
       describe [;
           switch (sandwich_state) {
             SW_UPSTREAM: "^Offshore, a thin man in sandwich boards sits in a
                 narrow gilded scull, eating a sandwich and watching the
                 ship.";
             SW_AHEAD, SW_WAITING: "^Sandwich sits on the wreck's upturned
                 side, a boathook across his knees, eating a sandwich.";
             SW_HOLDING:
                 print "^Sandwich sits on the wreck's upturned side, holding ";
                 if (child(self)) print (the) child(self);
                 " up for you to admire.";
             SW_INTAKE: "^Sandwich's scull is pinned against the screen next
                 to your ship. Sandwich sits in it, holding a funeral plaque
                 against his chest like a menu.";
           }
       ],
       description "A thin, cheerful Mingan in sandwich boards. The front one
           says FIRST! and the back one says THANKS FOR THE ALPHA. He is
           eating a sandwich. Everybody on the river calls him Sandwich,
           because he's always just in front of you and just behind you. He
           floated down the Verdance years ago and never went back: ~Why would
           I? Everything rolls downhill. I just wait at the bottom.~",
       greeting "He waves a sandwich at you.",
       no_answer "He taps his front board. FIRST!",
       before [;
         Take, Pull, Push: "He's already out of reach, smiling. Latency.";
       ],
       life [;
         Give:
           if (noun == coat) return SandwichTakesCoat();
           "~Floor price: zero. Pass.~";
         Show:
           "~Floor price: zero. Pass.~";
         Attack, Kiss:
           "He's already out of reach, smiling. Latency.";
       ],
  has  male proper transparent;

! The front-run rule, as an action. Called from the Wreck's `before` for
! every TAKE there while he is present; returning false lets the take go on.
[ SandwichGrab item;
    if (item ~= sweep or grapnel or strongbox or nameboard) rfalse;
    if (sandwich_state == SW_HOLDING) {
        if (item in sandwich) "He's already out of reach, smiling. Latency.";
        if (item ~= strongbox) rfalse;      ! the rest are yours for the taking
        move child(sandwich) to wreck;
        print "~Better offer!~ He drops what he was holding, hooks the
            strongbox instead, and tucks it under one arm.";
        return SandwichRowsOff(true);
    }
    if (sandwich_state ~= SW_WAITING) rfalse;
    if (item == strongbox) {
        print "His boathook flicks past your hand, and he has the strongbox
            first. ~FIRST!~ He tucks it under one arm.";
        return SandwichRowsOff(true);
    }
    move item to sandwich;
    sandwich_state = SW_HOLDING;
    print_ret "His boathook flicks past your hand, and he has ", (the) item,
        " first. ~FIRST!~ He holds it up and smiles. ~Yours for the coat.~";
];

! GIVE COAT TO SANDWICH: while he holds a decoy, the trade. It always works,
! and it never scores P2.
[ SandwichTakesCoat;
    if (sandwich_state ~= SW_HOLDING) "He looks at the coat the way a man
        looks at a price. ~Not yet, chief.~";
    print "He hands you ", (the) child(sandwich), " and takes the coat, and
        puts it on over his boards. You paid the front-runner. Everyone
        does.";
    move child(sandwich) to player;
    remove coat;
    return SandwichRowsOff(false);
];

! He leaves the Sandbar: the beached scull goes with him, and he goes ahead
! to the intake, where he will be pinned with the plaque (Part Five).
[ SandwichRowsOff with_box;
    if (with_box) {
        remove strongbox;
        Achieved(TASK_FEINT);
    }
    remove beached_scull;
    move sandwich to intake_screen;
    move sutt_plaque to sandwich;
    sandwich_state = SW_INTAKE;
    print " He rows away downstream, and is gone from the Sandbar.^";
    rtrue;
];

! P9's payoff: the pump stops, the screen lets him go, he pays up.
[ SandwichPaysUp;
    move sutt_plaque to player;
    remove sandwich;
    sandwich_state = SW_RACING;
    print "^Sandwich's scull comes loose from the slackening screen. He looks
        at the switch, and at you, and tosses you the plaque. ~Fair's fair.
        You front-ran the front-runner.~ Then he pushes off and rows away
        downstream, to be first at the bottom.^";
];

! SandwichTick: his schedule, at the end of the turn, after Lucan.
[ SandwichTick;
    ! Passage 1, drift 1: he shoots past, to be at the Wreck first.
    if (sandwich_state == SW_UPSTREAM && passage == 1 && drift == 1) {
        move sandwich to wreck;
        sandwich_state = SW_AHEAD;
        print "^A narrow gilded scull shoots past downstream, oars flashing.
            The man in it has FIRST! painted on his chest. ~FIRST!~^";
        return;
    }
    ! Your first entrance at the Wreck: he says the rule out loud.
    if (sandwich_state == SW_AHEAD && location == wreck) {
        sandwich_state = SW_WAITING;
        print "^Sandwich looks up from his sandwich. ~Morning, chief. Here's
            how it works down here. Whatever you reach for, chief, I'll have
            reached for it first. Nothing personal. It's latency.~^";
        return;
    }
    ! At the intake, once: he asks after Lucan. (`general` is a spare
    ! attribute every object has; here it means "has asked".)
    if (sandwich_state == SW_INTAKE && location == intake_screen &&
        sandwich hasnt general) {
        give sandwich general;
        print "^~Where's your tenor?~ Sandwich asks, and then looks at your
            face. A beat. ~Oh.~^";
        return;
    }
    ! The Race, drift 2: he goes past you, into the white water.
    if (sandwich_state == SW_RACING && passage == 5 && drift == 2) {
        sandwich_state = SW_GONE;
        print "^Sandwich goes past you like a thrown knife, both oars up,
            shouting ~FIRST ACROSS!~, and into the white water ahead. A moment
            later his scull breaks up on the rocks.^";
    }
];

! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  MRS. THRUMBLE, Clerk of Lost Property
! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  LESSON: a stationary service NPC. She is only in play while you are at
!  the pier and have rung: RING BELL moves her in, leaving the pier removes
!  her (the Stone Pier's `before`). She never looks up.
! ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Person thrumble "Mrs. Thrumble"
  with name 'mrs' 'thrumble' 'clerk' 'woman',
       describe [;
           "^Behind the bars of the hatch, Mrs. Thrumble is writing in a
           ledger. She does not look up.";
       ],
       description "A small grey woman in a cardigan, behind bars, writing in
           a ledger. She does not look up.",
       greeting "~Lost property, dear. Downstream counter.~ She doesn't look
           up.",
       life [;
         Ask, Tell, Answer:
           return ThrumbleTopic();
         Give, Show:
           "~Is it lost? Then it isn't mine to take, dear.~";
         Attack, Kiss:
           "There are bars in the way, and she is busy.";
       ],
  has  female proper;

! Her topic table: the same pattern as Lucan's.
[ ThrumbleTopic;
    if (AskedAbout('ascendancy', 'mingus', 'empire'))
        "~I'm sorry, I don't know what you're referring to.~";
    if (AskedAbout('mountain', 'above', 'sky'))
        "~I wouldn't know, dear. I don't look up.~";
    if (AskedAbout('ship', 'funeral'))
        "~Another one. They do keep coming.~";
    if (AskedAbout('lucan', 'tenor')) {
        if (lucan_state == DEAD) return ThrumbleTowelLine();
        "~I don't know the gentleman.~";
    }
    if (AskedAbout('sandwich', 'boards'))
        "~A gentleman in boards? He's never once rung. He shouts.~";
    if (AskedAbout('explorers', 'lost', 'people'))
        "~People do turn up from time to time, soaked and blue with cold. We
        give them a towel and a cup of tea, and they generally decide to
        stay.~";
    if (AskedAbout('river', 'verdance'))
        "~It goes on, dear. They do.~";
    "~Is that all? I've a queue.~";
];

[ ThrumbleTowelLine;
    "~We had a gentleman come down the old drainage shaft just now. I'm afraid
    he was past a towel, dear.~";
];

! RING BELL. The first ring after Lucan's death hands over his songbook.
[ ThrumbleRing;
    if (thrumble in stone_pier) "~She's already here, dear.~ She doesn't
        look up.";
    move thrumble to stone_pier;
    print "Footsteps on a ladder, somewhere below. The shutter behind the
        bars slides up, and a small grey woman in a cardigan opens a ledger.
        She does not look up. ~Lost property. Downstream counter.~^";
    if (lucan_state == DEAD && ~~songbook_given) {
        songbook_given = true;
        move songbook to player;
        print "^Then she pushes a small battered book through the bars.
            ~We had a gentleman come down the old drainage shaft just now.
            I'm afraid he was past a towel, dear. He'd want somebody to have
            it, I expect. Sign here.~ You sign. It is Lucan's songbook.^";
    }
    rtrue;
];

! N3: his songbook, off-stage until she hands it over.
LostName songbook "Lucan's songbook"
  with name 'lucan^s' 'songbook' 'book' 'battered',
       task TASK_SONGBOOK,
       reading "L. Fairweather",
       description "A small battered songbook. Inside the cover: L. FAIRWEATHER
           -- THURSDAYS. On the first page, in his hand: ~Where the green bank
           goes down to the water, there's a door that was never a door.~",
  has  proper;


! ============================================================================
!  12. THE DISPATCHER  --  the one end-of-turn daemon
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  world_clock is the only daemon in the game, and nothing else prints at the
!  end of a turn. Every turn that passes time runs it once: first the river,
!  then Lucan, then Sandwich, then it remembers where you are. Meta commands
!  (SCORE, SAVE, UNDO...) and commands the parser rejects pass no time, so
!  they never run it; everything else does -- LOOK, EXAMINE, INVENTORY,
!  WAIT, ASK, and refused actions included.
! ============================================================================

Object world_clock "world clock"
  with daemon [;
           RiverTick();
           if (deadflag) return;      ! the river may end the game
           LucanTick();
           SandwichTick();
           last_location = location;
       ];

! The passages' lines, by passage and drift. Drift 3 is the landing (or the
! window); RiverTick decides what happens after it.
[ PassageLine;
    switch (passage * 10 + drift) {
      11: print "The current turns the ship's high prow downstream and takes
              it, quick and quiet.";
      12: print "The Mingus bank slides by, and the verdlings on it, still
              bowed.";
      13: print "The ship runs up onto a long pale sandbar and stops: the way,
              by the look of it, they all do.";
      21: print "The ship swings out into the main stream. Ahead, the old-side
              bank begins to lean closer.";
      22: print "The old-side bank is near now: green, quiet, low. Something
              like a pier shows ahead.";
      23: print "The old-side bank is alongside: a stone pier, an iron ring. If
              you are going to steer for it, steer east now.";
      31: print "The current picks up. Far ahead, the gilded span reaches out
              over the river from the Mingus bank, and stops. A chain hangs
              from its broken end.";
      32: print "The broken end of the span is close now, and the chain hangs
              from it into the current, swinging.";
      33: print "The broken end is overhead, and the chain hangs from it, dead
              ahead and coming fast. Throw now or not at all.";
      41: print "The ship swings away under the broken end and runs on
              downstream, toward the Mingus bank.";
      42: print "A great gilded grating rises out of the water ahead, on the
              Mingus bank, and the river is bending toward it.";
      43: print "The intake pulls the ship sideways, gently, then not gently,
              against the grating. PUMPING STATION No. 2 -- INTAKE, says the
              gilt above you. The ship is pinned.";
      51: print "The ship comes off the screen, and the Verdance takes it,
              faster than before. White water ahead. Every song about this
              stretch ends the same way: hold on to something that floats --
              and it had better be on you.";
      52: print "The river steepens. Rocks show white on both sides.";
          if (lifebelt hasnt worn) print " If you are going to put that
              lifebelt on, now would be the time.";
      53: print "The river narrows to a throat of white water, and the old-side
              bank comes at you sideways, all rock. The high prow goes first.
              Then everything does.";
    }
];

! RiverTick: the current. Exact rules (the spec's, in code order):
!  1. Pinned at the intake, pump stopped, you aboard: the ship comes off the
!     screen (passage 5's cast-off) -- and step 3 runs on the same turn.
!  2. A window that was open last turn (drift 3 on passage 2 or 3) and you
!     didn't act: the eddy brings you round (2) or the broken end kills (3).
!  3. Adrift: drift + 1, print that drift's line and the counter. Drift 3
!     on passages 1, 4 and 5 lands automatically.
[ RiverTick;
    if (ship_at == INTAKE && pump_stopped && location ofclass ShipRoom)
        CastOff(5);
    if (ship_at ~= ADRIFT) return;
    if (drift == 3) {
        if (passage == 2) {
            drift = 1;
            print "^The pier slides past. Behind the point the river turns
                back on itself, and the eddy brings you round for another
                try.^";
            PrintCurrent();
            return;
        }
        if (passage == 3) {
            print "^The current takes the ship under the broken end, where
                everything that ever fell off the Gilded Span is waiting just
                under the surface. The hull opens like a letter.^";
            deadflag = 1;
            return;
        }
    }
    drift++;
    new_line;
    PassageLine();
    new_line;
    PrintCurrent();
    if (drift < 3) return;
    switch (passage) {
      1: ShipTo(SANDBAR);
      4: ShipTo(INTAKE);
      5: TheWreck();
    }
];


! ============================================================================
!  13. FINALE: THE OLD-SIDE SHORE  (room 20)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  Reached only by the wreck. Wearing the lifebelt, you are carried ashore
!  and the game ends with the win (deadflag 3); without it, the river.
! ============================================================================

[ TheWreck;
    if (lifebelt hasnt worn) { new_line; RiverDeath(); return; }
    Achieved(TASK_RACE);
    PlayerTo(old_side_shore);
    deadflag = 3;
];

Room old_side_shore "The Old-Side Shore"
  with description [;
           print "You come up in the shallows with the cork ring under your
               arms, and the river lets go of you, the way it has never let
               go of anyone. You are on the old-side bank. Behind you, what
               is left of the funeral ship is breaking up on the rocks, and
               the current is taking the pieces south, quick and quiet. The
               custody chest has washed up beside you, lid shut, still saying
               NOTHING HAS EVER BEEN WITHDRAWN. In the reeds a painted board
               lies face up: FIRST! Of the man who wore it there is no sign.
               Somewhere upstream, a hat with a velvet band is waiting at the
               edge of a bright green. Across the water, very small, the
               lights of the Ascendancy are flickering, the way they always
               do. Behind you the bank is green and warm, and a few low
               chimney vents give off a steady light that never flickers.
               Somewhere up there, a long way upstream, is home. You have
               gone further down the Verdance than anyone. Nobody has ever
               come back. Somebody has to be first.^";
       ];

! How many names made it into the chest (for the teaser).
[ NamesInChest  o n;
    objectloop (o in custody_chest) if (o ofclass LostName) n++;
    return n;
];


! ============================================================================
!  14. ENTRY POINTS  --  routines the library calls if the game provides them
! ============================================================================

! Initialise runs once, before the banner. It must set `location`; anything
! it prints appears above the story's title. StartDaemon starts the
! dispatcher; its first run is at the end of your first command.
[ Initialise;
    location = gangplank_foot;
    last_location = gangplank_foot;
    move pike to player;
    move coat to player;
    StartDaemon(world_clock);
    lookmode = 2;                 ! VERBOSE: full descriptions on every visit
    print "^^Last night you came down the Mingus with Lady Vaunt's coat and a
        verdling's pike, and the verdlings of the valley floor bowed to you.
        They made you a lane to the water. At the end of it was a funeral
        ship.^^The verdlings are still bowed. They have been bowed all night.
        In the grey light the lane they made for you still runs to the
        water, and at the end of it the funeral ship rocks at the mouth of
        the Sluice, its gangplank down, its blank plaque polished on the
        mast. Nobody who has floated down the Verdance has ever come back.
        Nobody here is going to stop you trying.^";
];

! deadflag 3 (set by TheWreck) ends the game with our own words; deadflag 1
! (every death) gets the library's own "You have died".
[ DeathMessage;
    if (deadflag == 3) print "You have gone further than anyone";
];

! The library calls PrintRank straight after "...in N turns". It must
! return true, or the library adds a full stop of its own. A win is always
! Furthest Downstream, whatever the score; the teaser comes with it.
[ PrintRank  n;
    print ", earning you the rank of ";
    if (deadflag == 3) {
        print "Furthest Downstream.^^You have reached the end of MINGUS II:
            EVERYTHING ROLLS DOWNHILL. ";
        n = NamesInChest();
        switch (n) {
          0: print "No names are in the chest";
          1: print "One name is in the chest";
          2: print "Two names are in the chest";
          3: print "Three names are in the chest";
          4: print "Four names are in the chest";
          5: print "Five names are in the chest";
        }
        if (songbook in custody_chest) print "; one of them used to sing on
            Thursdays";
        print ". Somewhere downstream, a man in sandwich boards is telling
            nobody that he got there first.^^Coming next: MINGUS III: THE OLD
            SIDE. Nobody has ever come back. Somebody has to be first.^";
        rtrue;
    }
    if (score >= 75) "Front-Runner.";
    if (score >= 50) "Downstream Validator.";
    if (score >= 25) "Liquidity Provider.";
    "Ballast.";
];

! Names for FULL SCORE, one per task number in section 1.
[ PrintTaskName task;
    switch (task) {
      TASK_LAUNCH:    "launching the funeral ship";
      TASK_FEINT:     "front-running the front-runner";
      TASK_SWEEP:     "shipping the sweep";
      TASK_KEDGE:     "kedging off the Sandbar";
      TASK_CROSSING:  "crossing the Verdance";
      TASK_LIFEBELT:  "finding what the song was right about";
      TASK_CHAIN:     "catching the chain";
      TASK_KEY:       "fishing up the civic key";
      TASK_PUMP:      "stopping the pump";
      TASK_RACE:      "surviving the Race";
      TASK_REGISTER:  "keeping the launch register";
      TASK_NAMEBOARD: "keeping the Expedition's names";
      TASK_SONGBOOK:  "keeping Lucan's songbook";
      TASK_TAG:       "keeping the diver's tag";
      TASK_PLAQUE:    "keeping Councillor Sutt's plaque";
    }
];


! ============================================================================
!  15. GRAMMAR  --  the standard verbs, then the game's own
! ============================================================================

Include "Grammar";

! --- Talking: HELLO, HI, GREET LUCAN, SAY HELLO, SAY HELLO TO LUCAN -----------
! The std grammar's SAY line is `* topic 'to' creature`. 'first' puts these
! two lines ahead of it, so SAY HELLO never needs a TO. HelloSub greets the
! person you named, or the first one present (Lucan, then Sandwich, then Mrs.
! Thrumble), using that person's `greeting` property.
[ HelloSub p;
    p = noun;
    if (p == 0) {
        if (lucan in location) p = lucan;
        else if (sandwich in location) p = sandwich;
        else if (thrumble in location) p = thrumble;
    }
    if (p == 0 || ~~(p ofclass Person)) "Nobody answers. The river doesn't.";
    PrintOrRun(p, greeting);
    rtrue;
];

Verb 'hello' 'hi' 'greetings'
    *                                   -> Hello
    * creature                          -> Hello;

Verb 'greet' 'salute'
    * creature                          -> Hello;

Extend 'say' first
    * 'hello'/'hi'                      -> Hello
    * 'hello'/'hi' 'to' creature        -> Hello;

! WARN LUCAN (ABOUT THE PATCHES): Lucan's `before` waves it off.
[ WarnSub; "Nobody here is listening for a warning."; ];

Verb 'warn' 'caution'
    * creature                          -> Warn
    * creature 'about'/'of' topic       -> Warn;

! --- FOLLOW LUCAN / FOLLOW HIM ----------------------------------------------------
! A SCOPE ROUTINE as a grammar token. The parser calls FollowScope with
! scope_stage 2 to ask "what can be named here?": we add Lucan wherever he
! is, and return false so the usual scope is added too. That is how FOLLOW
! LUCAN can name someone who has walked out of sight.
[ FollowScope;
    switch (scope_stage) {
      1: rfalse;                      ! no multiple objects
      2: if (lucan_state == FOLLOWING or GUIDING or ARRIVED)
             PlaceInScope(lucan);
         rfalse;
      3: "You can't see anyone like that to follow.";
    }
];

[ FollowSub;
    if (noun == lucan) return FollowLucan();
    if (noun == sandwich) "Nobody follows Sandwich. Everybody arrives after
        him.";
    if (noun in location) "It's right here.";
    "You can't see where it went.";
];

Verb 'follow' 'chase' 'pursue'
    * scope=FollowScope                 -> Follow;

! --- The launch: BOW (P1) -----------------------------------------------------
[ BowSub;
    if (location == main_deck && ship_at == SLUICE) {
        if (lines_let_go) "They have let go already. The lever is yours.";
        lines_let_go = true;
        "You bow to the verdlings on the bank. A thousand small hands let go
        of the lines at once.";
    }
    if (location == gangplank_foot or sluice_mouth) "The verdlings bow back,
        lower. The drill wants the passenger aboard.";
    "You bow to nobody in particular. The river does not bow back.";
];

Verb 'bow' 'kneel' 'curtsey' 'curtsy'
    *                                   -> Bow
    * noun                              -> Bow
    * 'to'/'before' noun                -> Bow;

! BOARD SHIP, EMBARK SHIP: the funeral ship's `before` walks you aboard.
Verb 'board' 'embark'
    * noun                              -> Enter;

! --- Steering: STEER EAST, STEER FOR PIER (P5) ---------------------------------
! noun=ADirection takes a compass direction (the std grammar's own filter
! routine); the second line takes any words after FOR/TO/TOWARD, as a topic,
! so STEER FOR PIER works without a pier object in scope on the ship.
[ SteerSub;
    return Steer(noun == e_obj);
];

[ SteerForSub;
    return Steer(AskedAbout('pier', 'bank', 'ring', 'shore', 'east', 'side'));
];

[ Steer east;
    if (location ~= stern || ~~sweep_shipped) "You'd need to be at the stern,
        with something to steer with.";
    if (~~(east && ship_at == ADRIFT && passage == 2)) "The river has its own
        ideas about that.";
    if (drift < 3) "Not yet -- the bank is still too far.";
    ShipTo(PIER);
    Achieved(TASK_CROSSING);
    "No one crosses the Verdance. You lean on the sweep, and the ship turns
    its high prow toward the old side, and crosses. It bumps along a stone
    pier, and you make fast to an iron ring. Far behind you, on the Mingus
    bank, the verdlings have turned their backs.";
];

Verb 'steer' 'helm'
    * noun=ADirection                   -> Steer
    * 'for'/'to'/'toward'/'towards' topic -> SteerFor;

! --- Throwing, hooking, pulling: the grapnel and the pike ------------------------
! THROW GRAPNEL INTO RIVER and THROW GRAPNEL OVERBOARD (the std grammar has
! THROW X and THROW X AT Y; both are ##ThrowAt).
Extend 'throw'
    * held 'into'/'in'/'over' noun      -> ThrowAt
    * held 'overboard'/'astern'         -> ThrowAt;

! PULL BOX WITH PIKE (P8), HOOK BOX WITH PIKE, GET BOX WITH PIKE, and HOOK
! CHAIN WITH GRAPNEL (P7): one action, ##PullWith, which the box and the
! chain answer in their own `before`.
[ PullWithSub; "Nothing comes of it."; ];

Extend 'pull'
    * noun 'with' held                  -> PullWith;

Extend 'get'
    * noun 'with' held                  -> PullWith;

Verb 'hook' 'snag'
    * noun 'with' held                  -> PullWith;

! TURN SWITCH WITH KEY (P9) is UNLOCK, as UNLOCK SWITCH WITH KEY already is.
Extend 'turn'
    * noun 'with' held                  -> Unlock;

! UNHOOK / FREE / RELEASE GRAPNEL: the Span's cast-off.
[ UnhookSub; "It isn't hooked on anything."; ];

Verb 'unhook' 'free' 'release'
    * noun                              -> Unhook;

! UNTIE ROPE / UNTIE LINE / UNTIE SHIP / CAST OFF: the Pier's cast-off.
[ UntieSub; "There's nothing there to untie."; ];

Verb 'untie' 'unfasten' 'unmoor' 'unhitch'
    * noun                              -> Untie;

[ CastOffSub;
    if (ship_at == PIER && location == main_deck or stone_pier)
        <<Untie mooring_rope>>;
    "There's nothing here to cast off.";
];

Verb 'cast'
    * 'off'                             -> CastOff
    * 'off' noun                        -> CastOff;

! CLIMB DOWN CHAIN (the std grammar has CLIMB X and CLIMB UP/OVER X).
Extend 'climb'
    * 'down' noun                       -> Climb;

! --- The river: JUMP OVERBOARD, SWIM IN RIVER, STEP ON PATCH, WALK DOWN ROAD ----
Extend 'jump'
    * 'overboard'                       -> Jump;

Extend 'swim'
    * 'in'/'into' noun                  -> Swim;

Verb 'step'
    * 'on'/'onto'/'in'/'into' noun      -> Enter;

! `last`, so plain GO DOWN still means the direction.
Extend 'go' last
    * 'down'/'along' noun               -> Enter;

! --- Ringing and driving -------------------------------------------------------
[ RingSub; "Nothing rings."; ];

Verb 'ring' 'buzz'
    * noun                              -> Ring;

[ DriveSub;
    if (location == drowned_road) "You don't. You know exactly where it goes.";
    "There's nothing here to drive.";
];

Verb 'drive'
    *                                   -> Drive
    * noun                              -> Drive;
